 ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Lucioni M, D’Ascanio L, De Nardi E, et al. Management of paratracheal lymph nodes in laryngeal cancer with subglottic involvement. Head Neck 2018;40:24-33. https://doi.org/10.1002/hed.24905 10.1002/hed.24905 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-03 14:02:04','2025-10-03 14:02:04');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(436,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12245270','Surgical Management of Craniospinal Axis Solitary Fibrous Tumors: A Single-Institution Case Series and Comprehensive Review of the Literature.','Piscopo AJ, Chowdhury AJ, Teferi N, Lee S, Challa M, Petronek M, Eschbacher K, Bathla G, Buatti JM, Hitchon P.','2023','craniospinal supratentorial infratentorial symptomatically karnofsky craniospinal hemangiopericytoma eor gtr hemangiopericytoma karnofsky hemangiopericytomas hpcs 12q13 nab2 stat6 craniospinal gtr craniospinal karnofsky dotmatics supratentorial infratentorial d948 supratentorial infratentorial eor gtr eor gtr d949 d950 d951 d952 gtr peripancreatic karnofsky gtr reoperations eor eor eor eor eor eor nonmeningothelial whitish ovoid storiform fascicles collagenous hypercellularity staghorn 12q13 nab2 ngfi stat6 stat6 t10 haphazardly hemangiopericytomas hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytomas hemangiopericytoma macagno hyperdense calvarium d953 voids voids infratentorial intraparenchymal postgadolinium voids postgadolinium intradural extramedullary craniospinal abtah supratentorial infratentorial gtr eor gtr gtr intradural extramedullary intramedullary intraparenchymal gtr symptomatically gtr eor oncofunctional eloquent gtr kinslow gtr gtr eor eor gtr peritumoral eor eor gtr hemangiopericytoma trialed adriamycin succumbed pleomorphism eor gtr eor underpowering eor gtr piscopo piscopo piscopo uiowa ajmain uiowa nahom teferi nahom teferi uiowa uiowa meron challa meronah418 petronek petronek uiowa kathryn eschbacher kathryn eschbacher uiowa girish bathla bathla girish buatti buatti uiowa ul1tr002537 craniospinal craniospinal craniospinal convexity hyperdense compressing craniospinal reverted hemangiopericytoma metastasize hemangiopericytomas boasting peritumoral taipei craniospinal craniospinal standout tackled fared eagerly await jaehyun apra arbi knafo kalyana nab2 stat6 metellus bouvier guyotat jenson haymes hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytoma e10926 rutkowski hemangiopericytoma kinslow hemangiopericytoma ghose guha kundu tew hemangiopericytoma swaminathan ruzevick venur hemangiopericytoma reifenberger chmielecki crago nab2 stat6 wesseling hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytoma jaboin attia e2656 zagars demicco feig guadagnolo schuetze bolejack choy dasatinib chordoma tihan viglione rosenblum olivi hemangiopericytoma e629 e638 hyslop tetraplegia deangelis brandes rano agha sohrabi caseseries tariq din ghafar ronchi cozzolino zito extrapleural bisceglia galliani giannatempo mohajeri tayebwa nab2 stat6 nonrandom macagno vogels appay hemangiopericytomas marseille hemangiopericytoma weon byun nawashiro nagakawa osada meninges lyndon lansley evanson giordan marton guerriero canova hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytoma hemangiopericytoma justa allut santias haemangiopericytoma neurocirugia astur hemangiopericytoma krog hemangiopericytoma inbar mousavi hemangiopericytomas e60 e69 abtah murayi ejikeme hemangiopericytoma e190 e196 buerki horbinski kruser demicco maki pasquali gronchi hochholzer craniospinal craniospinal craniospinal convexity hyperdense compressing','Abstract BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES: Meningeal solitary fibrous tumors (SFTs) comprise 0.4% of primary central nervous system neoplasms and carry metastatic potential. Disease course and optimal management are largely unknown, and there is currently no literature rigorously describing neurological outcomes in surgically managed SFTs. We present one of the largest craniospinal SFT series, analyze patient outcomes, and extensively review the associated literature. METHODS: All surgically managed SFTs at our institution between January 2005 and March 2023 were retrospectively reviewed. Patient demographics, tumor and radiographic features, treatment, and clinical outcomes were collected. Neurological function was quantified using Frankel grade and Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology scores. Descriptive statistics, multivariate analysis, log-rank test, and Kaplan–Meier survival analysis were performed. RESULTS: Twenty-one patients satisfied inclusion criteria. Tumor locations included 15 supratentorial, three infratentorial, and three spinal. All patients underwent surgical resection, and 16 (76.2%) underwent radiation. Six (28.6%) patients had tumor recurrence, and three (14.3%) developed metastasis. Younger age and higher postoperative Frankel grade were significantly associated with increased overall survival (OS) ( P = .011, P = .002, respectively). All patients symptomatically improved or stabilized after surgery, and Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology score ( P = .001) and functional status significantly improved postoperatively (Karnofsky Performance Status: 65.2 ± 25.2 vs 91.4 ± 13.5, P = .001). Sex, adjuvant radiation, and extent of resection were not significantly associated with OS. CONCLUSION: SFT of the central nervous system is a rare entity with a variable clinical course. Surgical resection was associated with improved postoperative functional and neurological status. Higher postoperative neurological function was significantly associated with OS. Further studies are warranted to validate a standardized treatment algorithm and investigate the efficacy of adjuvant radiation in SFT. KEY WORDS: Craniospinal axis, Hemangiopericytoma, Mesenchymal tumors, SFT/HPC, Solitary fibrous tumor ABBREVIATIONS: CNS central nervous system EOR extent of resection GTR gross total resection HPC hemangiopericytoma KPS Karnofsky Performance Status NANO Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology score OS overall survival QoL quality of life Sc spinal cord SFT solitary fibrous tumor STR subtotal resection WHO World Health Organization. Solitary fibrous tumors (SFTs) are aggressive neoplasms that occur in a range of anatomic sites throughout the body, most commonly the pleura, with up to 20%–30% arising within the central nervous system (CNS). 1 - 3 Overall, SFTs are rare entities that represent 0.4% of primary CNS tumors and less than 1% of intracranial tumors, whereas spinal SFTs are even rarer with nearly 10 cranial cases for every one spinal SFT. 1 , 4 , 5 The overall age-adjusted incidence for SFTs is 3.77 per 10 000 000 individuals, with 23%–51% of tumors eventually demonstrating local recurrence or metastasis after initial treatment. 6 , 7 SFTs are primarily discovered in middle-aged patients, with a mean age of 44 years at presentation and no predilection regarding sex. 8 In 2016, the World Health Organization (WHO) issued a designation combining SFTs and hemangiopericytomas (HPCs) into a single entity because of the sharing of genetic origins, a chromosomal inversion at the 12q13 locus leading to a NAB2::STAT6 fusion product. 2 , 9 , 10 However, in the latest 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the CNS, the joint term “SFT/HPC” has been consolidated to “SFT.” 11 SFTs of the craniospinal axis may present with a variety of neurological symptoms based on location. 1 , 12 Surgical resection is considered the gold-standard treatment, and gross total resection (GTR) is typically the goal when feasible because of the significant risk of recurrence. 13 , 14 Several studies have also suggested that adjuvant radiotherapy may reduce the risk of recurrence in cases of subtotal resection (STR); however, its role remains controversial. 15 - 17 SFT of the CNS is a rare entity with only several hundred cases reported in the literature, most of which are case reports or small series. 18 , 19 As such, the natural disease course, long-term prognosis, optimal treatment options, and associated complications are largely unknown. Furthermore, there are no studies rigorously describing neurological or functional outcomes in surgically managed patients with SFTs because these are related to survival. The objectives of this study are to classify and analyze postsurgical patient functional and neurological outcomes, evaluate the efficacy of adjuvant therapy, and compare our outcomes with the available literature. METHODS The study was approved by the institutional review board, and a retrospective review of hospital records was performed for a pathological diagnosis of SFT, SFT/HPC, and HPC from January 2005 to March 2023. Patients with tumors outside of the craniospinal axis were excluded after manual review of their medical records. Chart records were obtained from the EPIC (Epic Systems Corporation) electronic medical record. Informed consent was waived by the Institutional Review Board for all subjects. Patient demographics, tumor characteristics, radiographic features, clinical course, treatment modalities, and neurological and survival outcomes were retrospectively collected from patient EMRs. Neurological status was documented with pre- and postoperative Frankel grades. 20 Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology (NANO) scores and Karnofsky Performance Statuses (KPS) were collected to document neurological and functional status. 21 Descriptive statistics were performed for patient demographics, tumor characteristics, clinical course, and treatment modalities. GraphPad Prism 9 (Dotmatics LLC) was used for quantitative analysis. Categorical variables were compared using the Fisher''s exact test and χ 2 test, and numerical variables were analyzed using the Mann–Whitney–Wilcoxon rank sum test. Multivariate logistic regression, log-rank, and Kaplan–Meier survival analyses were also performed. Overall survival (OS) was calculated from the date of initial surgery to the date of death or most recent follow-up as reported in patient electronic medical record. Progression-free survival (PFS) was calculated from the date of initial surgery to the first reported date of tumor recurrence or postoperative discovery of recurrent tumor on radiological evaluation or most recent follow-up (if no disease progression). This case series has been reported in line with the PROCESS (Preferred Reporting Of CasE Series in Surgery) Guideline and the ethical principles set forth by the Declaration of Helsinki. 22 RESULTS Patient Demographics Our initial query yielded 35 patients with SFTs, and 14 were excluded because of the location outside of the CNS. Twenty-one patients were included (8 males and 13 females). The mean age was 55.4 ± 18.0 years (Table 1 ). Eight patients harbored grade II SFTs, 12 patients had grade III SFTs, and one patient had a SFT of indeterminate grade on histological evaluation. Fifteen patients had supratentorial tumors, three were infratentorial, and three had spinal tumors (Table 1 , Supplemental Digital Content 1, Table 1, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D948 ]). TABLE 1. Survivor vs Deceased Demographic and Tumor Characteristics Variable Surviving, n (%) Deceased, n (%) a P value b Age (y) 15 (0.71) 6 (0.29) .014 <30 0 (0) 0 (0) 30–60 12 (0.57) 1 (0.05) >60 3 (0.14) 5 (0.24) Sex .999 Male 7 (0.33) 2 (0.10) Female 9 (0.43) 4 (0.19) Ethnicity .526 Non-Hispanic White 12 (0.57) 6 (0.29) Black 1 (0.05) 0 (0) Hispanic 1 (0.05) 0 (0) Asian 1 (0.05) 0 (0) WHO grade .642 Grade II 5 (0.24) 3 (0.14) Grade III 9 (0.43) 3 (0.14) Indeterminate 1 (0.05) 0 Location .999 Supratentorial 11 (0.52) 4 (0.19) Infratentorial 2 (0.10) 1 (0.05) Spinal 2 (0.10) 1 (0.05) EOR .184 GTR 14 (0.67) 4 (0.19) STR 1 (0.05) c 2 (0.10) Radiation therapy .624 Adjuvant 10 (0.48) d 1 (0.05) Salvage 3 (0.14) d 3 (0.14) Observation 3 (0.14) 2 (0.10) Radiation type d .999 Intensity-modulated radiation therapy 11 (0.52) 4 (0.19) Stereotactic radiosurgery 3 (0.24) 0 (0) Chemotherapy 0 1 (0.05) .999 Recurrence 4 (0.19) 2 (0.10) .354 Metastasis 1 (0.05) 2 (0.10) .184 Open in a new tab EOR, extent of resection; GTR, gross total resection; STR, subtotal resection; WHO, World Health Organization. a Three patients died because of recurrent disease and three because of treatment complications including neutropenic fever/septic shock because of chemotherapy, one because of severe radiation necrosis, and one with postoperative sagittal sinus thrombosis treated with heparin anticoagulation who developed a subsequent life-threatening hemorrhage. b Significance < .5 c Mitotic index and presence of necrosis not available in the pathology report to accurately assign grade. d Of the 12 surviving patients treated with radiotherapy, one received both intensity-modulated radiation therapy and stereotactic radiosurgery. The most common presenting symptom was gait abnormality (n = 8, 38.1%), followed by vision changes and cognitive difficulty (each n = 6, 28.6%), headache (n = 5, 23.8%), and weakness (n = 4, 19%) ( Supplemental Digital Content 2, Table 2, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D949 ). The average preoperative KPS was 65.2 ± 25.2, whereas the average postoperative KPS was 91.4 ± 13.5 ( P = .001) (Table 2 , Supplemental Digital Content 3, Figure 1, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D950 ). The average preoperative cumulative NANO score was 2.00 ± 1.34, whereas the postoperative cumulative NANO score was 0.75 ± 0.75, which was also statistically significant ( P = .001) (Table 2 , Supplemental Digital Content 4, Table 3, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D951 ], and Supplemental Digital Content 5, Table 4, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D952 ). Six patients developed local recurrence of SFT (two developed multiple recurrences), of which three developed distant metastases (Table 1 ). The average time to recurrence was 106.7 months, and GTR was performed in 5/6 cases. In the three cases of metastasis, sites included the cervical spine, a peripancreatic nodule, and a distal femur lesion with an average time to metastasis of 118 months. TABLE 2. Pre- vs Postoperative Neurological and Functional Status Scoring system Number (n) P value a Preoperative Frankel grade, n (%) .192 D 7 (0.33) E 14 (0.67) Postoperative Frankel grade, n (%) D 2 (0.10) E 19 (0.91) Preoperative KPS (SD) .001 Average score 65.2 (25.2) Median score 70.0 Postoperative KPS Average score 91.4 (13.5) Median score 91.4 NANO (cumulative) .001 Preoperative average 2.00 Postoperative average 0.75 Open in a new tab KPS, Karnofsky Performance Status; NANO, Neurologic Assessment in Neuro-Oncology score. a Significance < .5 Treatment Factors Eighteen patients in this cohort underwent GTR, and three patients underwent STR (Table 1 ). Four patients required reoperation, and one of these four required three reoperations for multiple recurrences. Postoperatively, 11 patients underwent adjuvant radiation therapy and six required salvage radiotherapy after disease recurrence or refractory disease. One patient was treated with chemotherapy (Table 1 ). Clinical Outcomes Univariate analysis showed a trend toward decreased recurrence with postsurgical adjuvant radiation therapy ( P = .10). On multivariate regression, a trend was noted between recurrent disease and tumor grade ( P = .10) and adjuvant radiation therapy ( P = .18) but was not significant for age, sex, or extent of resection (EOR). There was no statistically significant association between disease progression and adjuvant radiation therapy or EOR nor was there any significant association between EOR and local disease recurrence. Survival Analysis Six patients died because of their disease, three because of recurrent disease and three because of complications of treatment including radiation necrosis, neutropenic fever, and septic shock after chemotherapy and postoperative hemorrhage (Table 1 ). Kaplan–Meier analysis suggested that younger age ( P = .01) and higher postoperative Frankel score ( P = .002) were significantly associated with increased OS, with EOR and adjuvant radiation therapy also trending toward significance ( P = .19; 0.16, respectively) (Figure 1 ). Sex, tumor grade, and metastatic disease did not have a significant impact on survival (Figure 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Kaplan–Meier survival curves displaying association A , between age ( P = .01), B , postoperative Frankel grade ( P = .002), C , EOR ( P = .19), D , sex ( P = .26), E , adjuvant radiation ( P = .16), and F , tumor grade ( P = .58) and OS. EOR, extent of resection; OS, overall survival. DISCUSSION SFTs are rare mesenchymal neoplasms with metastatic potential. They are categorized by the 2021 WHO classification as mesenchymal, nonmeningothelial, and soft tissue tumors with reported tumors ranging in size from 1 to 40 cm. 23 Histological Appearance and Molecular Features Grossly, SFTs appear as well-circumscribed, whitish, firm masses that may be partially encapsulated with or without areas of necrosis/hemorrhage (Figure 2 ). 23 , 24 Histologically, SFTs are described as ovoid, spindle-shaped cells in a storiform configuration with randomly oriented fascicles in a thick collagenous, fibrous stroma with alternating areas of hypo- and hypercellularity (Figure 2 ). 23 Large blood vessels are often present that merge with the adjacent tumor in a characteristic “staghorn” pattern. 12 , 19 , 25 , 26 Molecularly, SFTs are characterized by a pathognomonic inversion of the long arm of chromosome 12 (12q13), resulting in fusion of two genes, NAB2 (NGFI-A binding protein 2) and STAT6. 2 This fusion is a distinct molecular feature of SFTs and is present in 100% of cases. 27 Immunohistochemistry shows positive staining for nuclear STAT6 in addition to CD34 and vimentin. 2 , 19 FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab A , Gross appearance of spinal SFT after dural opening and exposure. B , Spinal SFT, after resection, is solid and relatively firm with minimal prominence of vascularity. C and D , Sagittal pre- and postoperative T1-weighted MRI with gadolinium contrast images demonstrating a round T9-T10 hyperintense lesion in the spinal canal, respectively. Hematoxylin and eosin, E , 100× magnification: SFT with typical haphazardly arranged cells within a background of thin-walled, dilated vessels and F , 200× magnification: area of tumor necrosis supportive of CNS WHO grade III designation. Cd, caudal; Cr, cranial; D, dura mater; Sc, spinal cord; SFT, solitary fibrous tumor. The previous 2016 WHO classification combined SFTs and hemangiopericytomas into a combined “SFT/hemangiopericytoma” nomenclature and divided SFTs into grades 1, 2, and 3, with higher grades demonstrating increased cellularity, vascularity, lower collagen content, and increased mitoses on microscopy (previously “anaplastic hemangiopericytomas”). 4 , 11 However, the WHO 2021 CNS tumor classification scheme has replaced the previous hybrid term “SFT/hemangiopericytoma” for “SFT” and has a newly modified 3-tiered CNS grading scheme that is based on the level of mitotic activity and the presence of necrosis. 11 This was performed to expand flexibility in grading within tumor types, to conform more closely with WHO Blue Book on Bone and Soft Tissue Tumors soft tissue tumor pathology, and to reinforce similarities between tumors in distinct categories. 11 In our cohort, nearly all tumors were high grade (grade III [57.1%], grade 2 [38.1%]). This is distinct from the series reported by Macagno et al where 55% of patients were classified as WHO grade I but is comparable with the series by Su Sung et al, in which over 90% of tumors were high grade (grade 2 and 3). 28 , 29 Radiological Features Radiologically, noncontrast CT generally demonstrates an iso- to hyperdense extra-axial mass with regions of sparse calcification and lytic remodeling of overlying calvarium/bony spine ( Supplemental Digital Content 6, Figure 2, http://links.lww.com/NEU/D953 ). On MRI, lesions are generally heterogeneous on T2WI with regions of low and high signal, sometimes referred to as the “Yin-Yang” pattern. Along with prominent flow voids and avid heterogenous enhancement, this is most diagnostic and characteristic for SFTs (Figures 2 and 3 ) 30 , 31 Higher-grade lesions are more likely to show vascular flow voids, cystic changes, necrosis, and hemorrhage. Cranial SFTs tend to occur more frequently in the supra- rather than infratentorial region (Table 3 ). 32 Given that these lesions tend to be extra-axial and near the dura (with or without a dural tail) showing an elevated relative cerebral blood volume, a differential diagnosis of meningioma is often considered because 10/21 patients in our series had an initial diagnosis of meningioma on radiological examination. 3 , 23 , 33 Intraparenchymal cranial lesions are exceedingly rare. 34 FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab MRI of brain showing well-circumscribed extra-axial mass in the right frontal lobe with avid heterogeneous enhancement in A , axial T-1 postgadolinium and B , axial T2 sequences. Prominent flow voids are also seen in these areas (blue arrows). MRI of postgadolinium contrast T1 sequence of the spine showing an intradural extramedullary mass at T5-6 on C , axial and D , sagittal views of a different patient. TABLE 3. Literature Review of Large Craniospinal SFT Series Variable El-abtah et al 2023 42 Liu et al 2022 33 Jiang et al 2016 16 Patients 34 a 38 24 Male sex, n (%) 19/34 (0.56) 15/38 (0.39) 13/24 (0.54) Location, n (%) Supratentorial 22/31 (0.67) 24/38 (0.63) 15/24 (0.63) Infratentorial 6/31 (0.18) 14/38 (0.37) 5/24 (0.21) Spinal 5/31 (0.15) 0 4/24 (0.17) WHO grade, n (%) I 0 12/38 (0.32) 18 <4 mitoses/HPF d II 9/15 (0.60) 17 (0.45) 6 >4 mitoses/HPF d III 4/15 (0.27) 9 (0.24) 6 with necrosis d GTR, n (%) 20/25 (0.8) a 29/38 (0.76) 21/24 (0.88) Adjuvant radiotherapy, n (%) 12/25 (0.48) a 11/38 (0.29) 3/24 (0.13) Recurrence, n (%) 15/34 (0.44) 13/38 (0.34) 3/24 (0.13) Metastasis, n (%) 11/34 (0.32) 5/38 (0.13) 1/24 (0.04) 5-year OS, % c 86.9 87.5 Median follow-up, mo 89.7 66.6 b 58 Open in a new tab EOR, extent of resection; GTR, gross total resection; OS, overall survival; SFT, solitary fibrous tumor; STR, subtotal resection; WHO, World Health Organization. a In total, thirty-four patients were in the study, but only 25 received initial treatment at institution; total reflects the number of patients with available data per variable. b Mean (median not available). c Five-year OS not available in this study; the mean PFS was 54 and 23 months in those who underwent GTR and STR, respectively. d WHO grading not provided in this study; instead, atypical/malignant (16/24 patients) and benign (9/24 patients) classifications were used as determined by a neuropathologist. Spinal SFTs may arise from any region of the spine and are seen most commonly arising from the dura as an intradural, extramedullary lesion (Figure 3 ). However, intramedullary spinal SFTs have been reported and seem higher in prevalence than intraparenchymal cranial SFTs. 19 , 35 - 37 Management and Long-Term Outcomes Given that most SFTs are localized at presentation yet harbor a significant risk of recurrence/metastasis, complete GTR with negative surgical margins is the goal of treatment with previous reports supporting use of adjuvant radiation for high-grade lesions to minimize recurrence. 38 Although there is widespread agreement regarding surgical intervention, to date, there have been no studies rigorously investigating functional and neurological outcomes pre- and postoperatively in patients with SFT as related to survival. This question is paramount in determining the optimal course of management and likelihood of patient benefit from surgery while considering tumor burden, functional status, and patient quality of life (QoL). Yu et al 39 reported that QoL scores were significantly lower and emotional distress was significantly higher in patients with SFT who had increased tumor burden, disease progression, or metastasis. They proposed that adjuvant treatment (ie, stereotactic radiation) may be particularly beneficial in patients with STR or disease progression. 39 In our cohort, over 80% of patients remained stable or symptomatically improved after surgery, and functional status significantly improved postoperatively (preoperative KPS: 65.2 ± 25.2 vs postoperative KPS 91.4 ± 13.5, P = .001). We further used Frankel grade and NANO scores to classify neurological status and found that NANO score improved significantly after surgery ( P = .001) and that higher postoperative Frankel grade was associated with increased OS ( P = .002). This seems to suggest that improvements in neurological status (specifically chronic pain, weakness, the ability to walk, and vision), which undoubtedly contribute to QoL, are associated with improved patient survival. Similarly, younger age (<60 years) was noted to correlate with improved OS ( P = .01), which could be attributed to the likelihood of improved functional status in these patients, postoperatively. Given the small number of patients in each group, further subgroup analysis was not feasible. GTR is the gold-standard treatment for SFT, and EOR has been suggested to be associated with improved OS (trend toward significance in our series, P = .19). 13 , 26 , 40 However, this must be weighed against the inherent risks of surgery including new neurological deficits, infection, hemorrhage, pain, and a significant recovery period. Considering these risks, a goal of “oncofunctional” balance should be made with maximal but safe tumor resection in mind. In our series, three patients underwent STR because of tight adhesions or invasion of eloquent surrounding neurovascular structures. In one case of an SFT invading the frontal sinus, orbit, and orbital apex, the tumor was noted tightly adherent to the ipsilateral optic nerve and a small attachment was left along the nerve after blunt dissection, debulking, and nerve decompression. STR was also performed in a second case of a large, >8-cm lesion demonstrating significant compression and invasion of the cerebellum, brainstem, and temporal lobe requiring over 15 hours of surgery to achieve adequate debulking. Empiric data demonstrate the need to balance oncological disease control with functional status, QoL, and neurological outcomes in the decision-making process, and poor functional and neurological status has also been shown to correlate with decreased OS in our series. 1 , 3 , 7 , 34 , 39 The benefits of adjuvant radiation for SFTs have historically been controversial but were recently corroborated in a study by Lee et al, 38 suggesting that postoperative radiotherapy (consisting of both fractionated and SRS treatment paradigms) significantly improved local disease control and PFS in both GTR and STR of grade 2 and 3 tumors. Kinslow et al 6 also demonstrated that in the setting of GTR, the addition of radiation therapy was associated with increased survival and conferred additional benefit over GTR alone. This study did not specify the exact modality of radiation therapy rendered, but Sheehan et al recently demonstrated in a multi-institutional study that SRS provides a reasonable rate of local tumor control with a low risk of adverse effects in the postsurgical setting of SFTs. 41 Kung et al suggested tumor aggressiveness as a predictor of treatment response because EOR and adjuvant radiotherapy were significantly associated with increased PFS in WHO grade 2 SFTs but not in more benign, slow-growing WHO grade 1 tumors. 29 In our cohort of 21 patients, three patients had metastasis, six had recurrences, and six opted to receive salvage radiation therapy. Overall, treatment with adjuvant radiation showed a trend toward decreased disease recurrence/progression ( P = .18) and increased survival ( P = .15) (Figure 1 ), whereas more aggressive EOR also showed a trend toward increased OS ( P = .19). Our relatively low recurrence rate supports previous reports that GTR plus adjuvant radiotherapy is a robust treatment strategy for SFTs of the CNS in preventing disease progression and improving survival, especially in cases of higher-grade pathology and/or STR. 5 , 7 , 38 , 39 , 42 - 44 Because SFTs are often mistaken for meningiomas on imaging, it is important to consider differences in treatment and prognostic implications between the two diagnoses. SFTs harbor a greater risk for local recurrence and metastasis, whereas most (81.1%) of the meningiomas are benign grade I lesions with markedly diminished risk of local recurrence. 45 The two diagnoses cannot be reliably distinguished on imaging although meningiomas more often demonstrate a dural tail sign. Importantly, the gold standard for a definitive diagnosis is histology and immunohistochemistry because the two entities derive from different cells of origin and pathological molecular alterations. Surgery is not always necessary in the setting of meningioma because indications include medically refractory symptoms (ie, seizure), significant growth on serial imaging, and more than minimal associated peritumoral edema. While there are no definitive guidelines regarding adjuvant radiotherapy for meningiomas, it has been suggested as potentially useful in cases of high-risk meningiomas (anaplastic WHO grade III regardless of EOR, new atypical WHO grade II meningiomas with STR, recurrent atypical meningiomas regardless of EOR) and new atypical meningiomas (WHO grade II) with GTR. Several studies have suggested that chemotherapy is of little benefit, and it is not currently first-line treatment for SFT of the CNS but has been suggested as a reasonable option to trial in cases of recurrent, intractable disease. 15 - 17 In our series, one patient with an original diagnosis of an aggressive, malignant hemangiopericytoma was trialed on combination cyclophosphamide, adriamycin, and vincristine. However, this patient eventually progressed and developed subsequent bony metastasis to the lower extremity and eventually succumbed to septic shock because of neutropenic fever. Interestingly, we did not note any significant association between sex or tumor grade and OS. SFT has not been associated with any particular patient demographics as risk factors for tumor development, recurrence, or decreased survival. 23 , 34 In contrast to many published reports, one recent study was the first to report an association between male sex and decreased PFS. 44 Several risk stratification models have been proposed attempting to categorize the risk of metastasis in SFTs. 46 , 47 Although the overall risk remains low, there is general agreement that risk factors for metastasis include older age at presentation, tumor size, higher-grade tumors (high mitotic activity, tumor necrosis, nuclear pleomorphism, and infiltrative borders), and STR in the case of previous surgery. 23 , 24 , 48 In our series of patients, there was no significant association between progression to metastatic disease and adjuvant radiation therapy, EOR, and tumor location or grade. Based on our institutional experience and literature review, we recommend maximal safe tumor resection of SFTs when feasible while balancing the need for oncological disease control with functional status, QoL, and neurological outcomes in the surgical decision-making process. In addition, there are now multi-institutional data to support the use of adjuvant radiotherapy after GTR and STR for patients with high-grade tumors (grade II and III) given the inherent high risk of recurrence. While there was a trend toward significance regarding EOR and adjuvant radiation with improvement in OS in our patient cohort ( P = .19, P = .15, respectively), neither of these variables achieved statistical significance, potentially because of the underpowering of the study owing to the disease’s overall rarity. Large-scale, multicenter randomized controlled clinical trials are warranted before standard-of-care treatment algorithms are established. In the interim timeframe, a multidisciplinary team discussion involving a panel of experts is recommended for guiding an optimal treatment strategy for individual patients. Limitations This institutional review was a retrospective study that included a limited number of patients from a single center. Given the rarity of the pathology and the expected single-institutional limited number of patients, we reviewed the literature extensively to further strengthen our recommendations. Because of the nonrandomized, retrospective nature of this investigation, its level of evidence is limited without larger, multicenter clinical trials to support and validate recommendations for optimal management. Future large-scale, prospective studies are warranted to elucidate the nuances and standardize treatment algorithms for this rare pathology. CONCLUSION Surgical management of SFTs of the CNS was associated with improved postoperative functional status and NANO scores. Higher postoperative neurological function and younger age were significantly associated with improved OS, whereas greater EOR and postoperative adjuvant radiotherapy showed a trend toward significance. Maximum tumor resection, when feasible, is a viable, safe treatment option and is recommended. Adjuvant radiotherapy was also associated with decreased recurrence risk and may be considered in cases of STR, GTR of high-grade lesions, local recurrence, or metastasis. Further studies are warranted before standardized treatment algorithms can be established. Footnotes Supplemental digital content is available for this article at neurosurgery-online.com . * Anthony J. Piscopo and A. J. Chowdhury contributed equally to this work. Contributor Information Anthony J. Piscopo, Email: anthony-piscopo@uiowa.edu. A. J. Chowdhury, Email: ajmain-chowdhury@uiowa.edu. Nahom Teferi, Email: nahom-teferi@uiowa.edu. Sarah Lee, Email: sarah-j-lee@uiowa.edu. Meron Challa, Email: meronah418@gmail.com. Michael Petronek, Email: michael-petronek@uiowa.edu. Kathryn Eschbacher, Email: kathryn-eschbacher@uiowa.edu. Girish Bathla, Email: bathla.girish@mayo.edu. John M. Buatti, Email: john-buatti@uiowa.edu. Funding Research reported in this publication was supported by the National Center For Advancing Translational Sciences of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number [UL1TR002537]. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. Disclosures The authors have no personal, financial, or institutional interest in any of the drugs, materials, or devices described in this article. Supplemental Digital Content 1, Table 1. Characteristics of all craniospinal tumors included in our cohort including grade, laterality, location, initial radiologic diagnosis, recurrence, and metastasis. Supplemental Digital Content 2, Table 2. Initial patient presenting symptoms leading to discovery of craniospinal SFTs on imaging for 21 patients in our cohort. Supplemental Digital Content 3, Figure 1. Pre- vs postoperative (A) KPS and (B) NANO scores for all patients presenting with craniospinal SFTs in our series. Supplemental Digital Content 4, Table 3. Individual components of pre- and postoperative NANO scores for all included patients. Supplemental Digital Content 5, Table 4. Pre- and postoperative NANO scores stratified by presenting symptoms leading to initial SFT diagnosis of all patients included in our cohort. Supplemental Digital Content 6, Figure 2. Axial CT of brain showing a right frontal convexity iso-hyperdense mass causing a mass effect with midline shift and compressing the right lateral ventricle. COMMENTS The article titled “Surgical Management of Craniospinal Axis Solitary Fibrous Tumors: A Single-Institution Case Series and Comprehensive Review of the Literature” aimed to ascertain the effectiveness of surgical treatment in controlling tumors, improving survival rates, and identifying predictive factors for SFTs. The 2021 WHO Classification of CNS Tumors reverted to using the term “SFT” instead of “hemangiopericytoma” and introduced a novel grading system based on mitotic activity and necrosis. SFTs have the potential to metastasize, resulting in severe neurological symptoms and mortality. The study addressed this gap by conducting a comprehensive analysis of evidence, evaluating neurological outcomes, and highlighting recent WHO changes and treatment outcomes. In the past, hemangiopericytomas were frequently compared with meningiomas. Given that SFTs often resemble meningiomas in imaging, it is imperative to differentiate between the two diagnoses and account for discrepancies in treatment approaches and prognostic implications. SFTs carry a heightened risk of local recurrence and metastasis, whereas the majority of meningiomas are categorized as benign grade I lesions, boasting significantly lower local recurrence rates. It is worth noting that histology and immunohistochemistry serve as the definitive diagnostic standards as these two entities originate from distinct cell origins and exhibit unique pathological molecular alterations. Surgery may not be universally indicated for meningiomas but usually necessary for SFTs. Surgical intervention is recommended in cases of medically refractory symptoms (such as seizures), substantial growth observed through sequential imaging, and substantial peritumoral edema. While explicit guidelines for adjuvant radiotherapy in SFTs are lacking, there is a suggestion that it could be beneficial, just like high-risk meningiomas with anaplastic features (WHO grade III), newly diagnosed atypical meningiomas (WHO grade II) with subtotal resection, and recurrent atypical meningiomas, among others. Lastly, due to their benign nature and clear margins, stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS) emerges as the optimal choice for treating SFTs, offering precision and focused treatment capabilities. In a recent multicenter study, Sheehan et al demonstrated that SRS provides a satisfactory level of local tumor control with minimal risk of adverse effects when employed in the postsurgical management of SFTs. Cheng-chia Lee Taipei, Taiwan The manuscript titled “Surgical Management of Craniospinal Axis Solitary Fibrous Tumors: A Single-Institution Case Series and Comprehensive Review of the Literature” offers a detailed exploration of surgical treatments for craniospinal axis solitary fibrous tumors (SFTs). A standout feature of this research is its thorough analysis of SFTs tackled over a remarkable 18-year span. Through comprehensive statistical methods and a retrospective approach, the study helps us understand the patients, the treatments they received, and how they fared afterward. The updated WHO classifications ensure it aligns with contemporary understandings of CNS tumors, making it a timely and essential read. The significance of your work lies in its novel findings on surgical outcomes for CNS SFTs, shedding light on the previously understudied relationship between surgery, neurological outcomes, and patient survival on patients with CNS SFTs. This work has the potential to influence future treatment guidelines. Your spotlight on the existing gaps in knowledge, particularly regarding the utility of adjuvant radiotherapy, offers a clear pathway for future research in this area. In summary, this manuscript is pivotal for those seeking to understand CNS SFTs. The depth of your findings holds the promise of informing future clinical guidelines. I eagerly await its publication and salute your in-depth research and insights. David Jaehyun Park Palo Alto, California, USA REFERENCES 1. Apra C, El Arbi A, Montero AS, Parker F, Knafo S. Spinal solitary fibrous tumors: an original multicenter series and systematic review of presentation, management, and prognosis. Cancers (Basel). 2022;14(12):2839. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Robinson DR, Wu YM, Kalyana-Sundaram S, et al. Identification of recurrent NAB2-STAT6 gene fusions in solitary fibrous tumor by integrative sequencing. Nat Genet. 2013;45(2):180-185. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Metellus P, Bouvier C, Guyotat J, et al. Solitary fibrous tumors of the central nervous system: clinicopathological and therapeutic considerations of 18 cases. Neurosurgery. 2007;60(4):715-722; discussion 722. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Jenson M, Haymes D, Patel J. Grade III so