eport. Pan Afr Med J. 2014;19:291–299. doi: 10.11604/pamj.2014.19.291.5320. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Are C, Berman RS, Wyld L, et al. Global curriculum in surgical oncology. Ann Surg Oncol. 2016;23:1782–1795. doi: 10.1245/s10434-016-5239-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Gruen RL, Pitt V, Green S, et al. The effect of provider case volume on cancer mortality: systematic review and meta-analysis. CA Cancer J Clin. 2009;59:192–211. doi: 10.3322/caac.20018. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Wyld L, Audisio RA, Poston GJ. The evolution of cancer surgery and future perspectives. Nat Rev Clin Oncol. 2015;12(2):115–124. doi: 10.1038/nrclinonc.2014.191. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Stephens MR, Lewis WG, Brewster AE, et al. Multidisciplinary team management is associated with improved outcomes after surgery for esophageal cancer. Dis Esophagus. 2016;19(3):164–171. doi: 10.1111/j.1442-2050.2006.00559.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Meguid C, Ryan CE, Edil BH, et al. Establishing a framework for building multidisciplinary programs. J Multidiscip Healthc. 2015;8:519–526. doi: 10.2147/JMDH.S96415. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Taylor C, Shewbridge A, Harris J, et al. Benefits of multidisciplinary teamwork in the management of breast cancer. Breast Cancer. 2013;5:79–85. doi: 10.2147/BCTT.S35581. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Hsu YH, Kung PT, Wang ST, et al. Improved patient survivals with colorectal cancer under multidisciplinary team care: a nationwide cohort study of 25,766 patients in Taiwan. Health Policy. 2016;120(6):674–681. doi: 10.1016/j.healthpol.2016.04.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Stefan DC, Masalu N, Ngendahayo L, et al. Pathology and oncology in Africa: education and training for the future in cancer research—East African Regional Meeting. Infect Agent Cancer. 2015;10:48–55. doi: 10.1186/s13027-015-0044-7. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Are C, Malik M, Patel A, et al. The training and certification of surgical oncologists globally. Ann Surg Oncol. 2015;22(3):710–718. doi: 10.1245/s10434-014-4343-9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Lopez MS, Baker ES, Milbourne AM, et al. Project ECHO: a telementoring program for cervical cancer prevention and treatment in low-resource settings. J Glob Oncol. 2016;3(5):658–665. doi: 10.1200/JGO.2016.005504. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Lopez MS, Baker ES, Schmeler KM, et al. Building a comprehensive cancer education program to increase clinical capacity in Mozambique. Meeting Proc J Glob Oncol. 2017. pp. 22s–23s. [ DOI ] 23. Lara-Santos S. Take home message: PALOP School of oncology it’s a need. 2017. [16/03/18]. AORTIC 2017|KIGALI|7–10 NOVEMBER; 283 [ http://aorticconference.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/10/2017-AORTICAbstracts.pdf ] 24. ten Cate O. A primer on entrustable professional activities. Korean J Med Educ. 2018;30(1):1–10. doi: 10.3946/kjme.2018.76. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Grover S, Balogun OD, Yamoah K, et al. Training global oncologists: addressing the global cancer control problem. Front Oncol. 2015;5:80. doi: 10.3389/fonc.2015.00080. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',79,'2025-10-03 14:07:03','2025-10-03 14:07:03');
