avani davari negahdaripour karimzadeh foods8030092 s41388 mcquade gopalakrishnan aaz7015 bolte bjork nct03950635 20diet 20cancer aggfilters ketorein nct05119010 cond 20cancer messaoudene pidgeon polyphenol circumvents jtho elkrief capella camu camu prebiotic curroncol30090570 guarner nrgastro sakata butyricum sakata butyricum cbm588 chemoimmunotherapy cancers16010047 dizman meza bergerot ebrahimi meza cbm588 cabozantinib mrcc lba104 swog swog protocoldevelopmentreports 6fgsf5fadagsj5hhhshsf55gffa glitza yadegar yoseph monaghan e0006022 baruch youngster betzalel abb5920 davar dzutsev abf3363 vanderwalde bellasea kendra chom routy lenehan whj wolchok chiarion sileni nejmoa1709684 tsk kudaba elkrief duttagupta 1068p fmt fmt luminate s707 s708 reck ciccarese porcari buti lba77 fmt axitinib tacito s1264 tislelizumab fruquintinib renmin fruquintinib sintilimab everimmune oncobax nct05865730','Abstract The interplay between the human gut microbiome and the immune system has sparked growing interest in microbiome modulation as a therapeutic strategy in oncology. Preclinical studies have identified specific bacterial species linked to improved responses to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), leading to clinical investigations in melanoma, renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and non–small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The stool bacterial abundance of Ruminococcaceae, Akkermansia, and Bifidobacterium correlates with favorable clinical outcomes, whereas the disruption of the gut microbiome through antibiotics before or during ICI initiation is associated with higher rates of primary resistance and shorter survival. Biomarkers such as TOPOSCORE have been developed to better predict ICI benefits and estimate dysbiosis and treatment responses. Several microbiome-modulating strategies have shown potential in patients receiving treatment with ICIs—for instance, high dietary fiber intake may be linked to improved outcomes. As a separate strategy, certain probiotics appear to enhance clinical activity in early trials when incorporated into ICI-based regimens. Finally, fecal microbiota transplantation has shown safety and efficacy in ICI-refractory melanoma and yielded encouraging results in treatment-naïve patients with melanoma, NSCLC, and RCC. Although several compelling signals have been observed to date with microbiome manipulation, the field is lacking large, definitive randomized trials—these are indeed a prerequisite for any of the highlighted strategies to become a standard of care. The human microbiome is an ecosystem of microorganisms composed primarily of bacteria that symbiotically interact with the body. 1 The gut microbiome''s interplay with the immune system makes microbiome modulation a focus for treating solid malignancies responsive to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). 2 Common strategies to modulate the gut microbiome, including prebiotics, probiotics, and fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), have shown clinical activity in patients with advanced solid malignancies receiving ICIs. By contrast, other approaches, such as postbiotics and vitamin supplementation, have not demonstrated meaningful clinical benefit. 3 - 5 In this review, we critically appraised studies addressing the gut microbiome modulation combined with ICIs in patients with solid malignancies. Figure 1 summarizes the main microbiome modulation approaches, their limitations, and relevant ongoing clinical trials in oncology. FIG 1. Open in a new tab Current strategies for microbiome modulation in cancer, their proposed mechanism of action, current limitations, and representative examples of ongoing clinical trials in solid malignancies. CRC: colorectal carcinoma; FMT, fecal microbiota transplant; HCC, hepatocarcinoma; ICI, immune checkpoint inhibitors; NCT, National Clinical Trial identifier number; NSCLC, non–small cell lung cancer; RCC, renal cell carcinoma; VEFG(R), vascular endothelial growth factor (receptor). THE MICROBIOME AND OUTCOMES WITH ICIs The adoption of ICIs as a cornerstone treatment for various tumors has fueled growing interest in the mechanisms underlying immunotherapy resistance. 6 In preclinical melanoma models, Sivan et al 8 and Vétizou et al 7 identified two commensal bacterial species, Bifidobacterium and Bacteroides, respectively, as potential modulators for ICI treatment responses. These discoveries paved the way for studies investigating the relationship between gut microbiome composition and patient outcomes with ICIs in solid malignancies, specifically advanced melanoma, metastatic renal cell carcinoma (mRCC), and metastatic non–small cell lung cancer (mNSCLC). Specific stool bacterial taxa, namely, Ruminococcaceae, Akkermansia, and Bifidobacterium, were associated with improved treatment responses to anti–PD-1 antibodies. 9 - 11 Additionally, data from the PURE-01 trial of neoadjuvant pembrolizumab for muscle-invasive bladder cancer showed that the Sutterella genus was more prevalent in ICI responders, and Ruminococcus bromii was enriched in nonresponders. 12 Additionally, the gut microbiome has been implicated in modulating responses to chemoradiation for rectal cancer, with butyrate-producing bacteria such as Roseburia, Dorea, and Anaerostipes being enriched in responders. 13 Although much of the early work has focused on the gut microbiome, emerging evidence suggests that the intratumoral microbiome may also play a direct role in shaping responses to cancer therapies. 14 Early studies in pancreatic cancer identified that intratumoral Gammaproteobacteria could hinder responses to gemcitabine. 15 Additionally, analysis of the intratumoral microbiota of patients with esophageal cancer and mNSCLC observed that the presence of Streptococcus and Escherichia in patients receiving neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy or systemic ICI, respectively, was associated with increased ICI responsiveness. 16 , 17 These insights into the microbiome''s impact, both within the tumor microenvironment and systemically through the gut, have prompted further investigation into how its disruption may affect treatment efficacy. Derosa et al 18 examined how gut microbiome disruption through antibiotics could negatively affect ICI outcomes. Antibiotic use before or concomitant with ICI initiation was associated with higher rates of primary progressive disease (PD) and shorter overall survival (OS). This prompted growing interest in identifying microbiome-based biomarkers of ICI response. For example, serum short-chain fatty acids, which are byproducts of butyrate-producing bacteria, have shown conflicting associations with outcomes and remain under investigation. 5 , 19 , 20 Similarly, although stool abundance of Akkermansia has been linked to ICI response, both low and excessively high levels appear suboptimal. This suggests that a balanced microbial composition may be more informative than the abundance of a single species. 22 This observation prompted the development of the TOPOSCORE, a predictive biomarker that integrates Akkermansia with two interacting microbial groups to estimate intestinal dysbiosis and the likelihood of ICI response. 23 Validation in cohorts of mNSCLC, mRCC, and metastatic urothelial carcinoma demonstrated a significant correlation between TOPOSCORE and OS, supporting the potential value of a dynamic scoring system. 23 MICROBIOME AS TREATMENT Role of Prebiotics in Cancer Treatment Prebiotics nourish beneficial gut microorganisms, enhancing postbiotic production and inhibiting pathogen colonization. 24 , 25 Spencer et al 26 studied the impact of dietary fiber and probiotics on patients with advanced melanoma receiving ICIs. The findings revealed that a higher dietary fiber intake significantly improved progression-free survival (PFS), particularly those consuming more than 20 g once daily without probiotics (hazard ratio [HR] for PD, 0.44). Bolte et al 27 also found a link between a Mediterranean diet and favorable outcomes in advanced melanoma receiving ICI treatment. Other interventions, such as the ketogenic diet, have shown increased ICI-induced cytotoxic T-lymphocyte activity in preclinical models, providing a rationale for the ongoing trials. 28 , 29 Natural substances may also serve as probiotics. Oral supplementation of camu camu, a castalagin-rich berry, in mice altered the gut microbiome composition, increasing the abundance of Ruminococcaceae and Alistipes, both associated with ICI responsiveness. 9 , 30 , 31 In a series by Pang et al, 32 two patients with advanced melanoma refractory to anti-PD1 exhibited deep clinical responses following the addition of camu camu extract to anti-PD1 rechallenge. Role of Probiotics in Cancer Treatment Probiotics are beneficial live microorganisms found in nature. 33 Preclinical data have been collected on the impact of specific live bacterial products on various solid malignancies; however, not all interventions have translated into improved clinical outcomes. Notably, Tomita et al 38 , 39 reported findings from two retrospective series highlighting the potential benefit of oral supplementation with C. butyricum MIYAIRI 588 (CBM588) in patients with mNSCLC receiving systemic treatment with either ICI or chemoimmunotherapy. In both cohorts, patients who received CBM588 during treatment experienced longer PFS and OS. This observation provided the rationale for two prospective randomized trials that added CBM588 to the standard of care in patients with mRCC. The first trial included 30 patients with intermediate- and poor-risk mRCC receiving first-line treatment with nivolumab and ipilimumab with or without CBM588. Patients in the experimental arm experienced significantly longer PFS (HR, 0.15) and trended toward a numerically higher overall response rate (ORR; 58% v 20%). 40 The second trial assessed 30 patients with mRCC receiving nivolumab and cabozantinib with or without CBM588. In line with the prior results, adding CBM588 to the standard treatment resulted in a significantly higher ORR (74% v 20%). 41 Notably, owing to the limited sample sizes, these findings should be interpreted cautiously, and widespread adoption of this strategy remains premature. However, the encouraging signals observed with CBM588, it is now being investigated in the phase III SWOG BioFront trial, which will evaluate its addition to frontline ICI-based therapy in patients with mRCC and may help define its role in standard oncologic care. 42 Although CBM588 represents a promising single-strain approach, broader strategies aimed at restoring gut microbial diversity have also been explored. One such example is SER-401, a spore-based formulation enriched with Firmicutes . 43 In a phase Ib trial, Glitza et al 43 randomly assigned 14 patients with advanced melanoma to nivolumab with SER-401 after preconditioning with vancomycin or placebo. The ORR was 25% in the experimental arm compared with 67% in the placebo arm. Translational analysis indicated that vancomycin preconditioning disrupted gut microbiota and impaired immunity, leading to incomplete recovery at ICI administration, lower ORR in the experimental arm, and prompting concerns about whether antibiotic preconditioning is truly beneficial in the context of microbiome-based interventions. Fecal Microbiota Transplantation FMT involves transferring fecal microbes from a healthy donor to modify the gut microbiota and potentially enhance ICI responses. 44 Two phase I trials evaluated the safety, feasibility, and activity of FMT combined with anti–PD-1 re-challenge in patients with advanced melanoma previously resistant to anti–PD-1 therapy, a setting in which ICI alone yields response rates below 10%. 45 - 47 Both phase I trials included a similar methodology, with FMT coming from donors who had previously been treated with an anti–PD-1 for metastatic melanoma and had achieved deep clinical responses. Baruch et al 45 included 10 patients who had presented PD on at least one anti–PD-1 agent. Participants received FMT in combination with nivolumab and reached an ORR of 30%, with one patient achieving a complete response (CR). On the other hand, Davar et al 46 included exclusively patients with anti–PD-1-refractory metastatic melanoma. A single donor-derived FMT was administered with pembrolizumab, followed by additional pembrolizumab therapy. Among the 15 patients who underwent the intervention, responses were observed in three patients, with one patient achieving a CR and another three having stable disease lasting over 12 months. Moreover, FMT has also been investigated as a frontline treatment. Routy et al 49 explored the activity of FMT and an anti–PD-1 in patients with metastatic melanoma in the first-line setting in the MiMic trial. This multicenter phase I clinical trial combined healthy donor FMT with either nivolumab or pembrolizumab in patients with treatment-naïve advanced melanoma. The ORR was 65%, and 20% of patients achieved a CR without any concerning toxicities. These results surpass historical benchmarks from the CheckMate 067 and KEYNOTE 006 trials. 50 , 51 The FMT-LUMINate trial builds on these findings, revealing encouraging results from combining FMT with ICIs in metastatic cases. 52 Designed as a phase II, multiarm study, FMT-LUMINate includes three distinct cohorts: patients with mNSCLC, advanced cutaneous melanoma, and uveal melanoma. The primary end point for all three arms was set to a prespecified ORR of 64%. Patients received FMT from healthy volunteers within 7 days before starting ICIs. From the 20 patients in the cutaneous melanoma arm, the ORR reached 75%, with four patients (20%) with CR. 52 In the mNSCLC arm of the trial, 20 patients with mNSCLC and a tumor proportion score ≥50% received first-line pembrolizumab with FMT. It has been reported that this group reached an ORR of 77%, pending final analysis. 52 These findings notably exceed the historical response rates observed in the KEYNOTE 042 and KEYNOTE 024 trials, underscoring the potential benefit of integrating FMT with immunotherapy. 51 , 53 Similar to the findings from CBM588, combining FMT with an anti-PD1 and antiangiogenic TKI in patients with mRCC has resulted in increased responses and extended survival. TACITO was a phase II randomized clinical trial evaluating the addition of FMT to standard first-line therapy for mRCC. 54 Fifty patients were randomized to receive pembrolizumab plus axitinib with either FMT or placebo. The trial met its primary end point, demonstrating a significant improvement in the 12-month PFS rate from 35% to 67%. Moreover, the ORR was nearly doubled in the FMT group compared with placebo (52% v 28%), highlighting the clinical activity of this approach. Although FMT has been evaluated in both refractory and frontline settings, its broader impact across the treatment continuum remains unclear, and the potential influence of microbiome modulation on subsequent therapy efficacy has yet to be fully explored. Building on its evaluation in melanoma and RCC, FMT has also been tested in other solid tumors resistant to ICIs. Kim et al 48 conducted a phase I, single-arm trial in patients with anti–PD-1-refractory upper gastrointestinal and hepatocellular cancers. Thirteen participants, all of whom had experienced PD on nivolumab, received oral antibiotics for microbiota depletion followed by FMT while continuing nivolumab. Donors were patients with solid tumors who had previously responded to PD-1 blockade. The ORR was 8%, and the DCR reached 46%. A similar approach was evaluated in the phase II, single-arm RENMIN-215 trial, which enrolled patients with microsatellite-stable metastatic colorectal cancer who had progressed after two prior lines of therapy. In this study, 20 patients received FMT in combination with tislelizumab (an anti–PD-1 antibody) and fruquintinib (an antiangiogenic TKI) as third-line treatment. The combination achieved a median PFS (mPFS) of 9.6 months and an ORR of 20% 55 However, when compared with historical data for fruquintinib plus another anti–PD-1 antibody in a similar setting, outcomes were comparable, suggesting that the addition of FMT may not have provided added clinical benefit. 56 These findings highlight the importance of carefully selecting the disease context when developing microbiome-based strategies. Future Directions Microbiome modulation holds great promise in cancer treatment, leveraging the gut-immune system interplay to enhance ICI efficacy. Although early data are encouraging, they remain insufficient to support routine clinical use, and large randomized trials are warranted to confirm benefit (Fig 1 ). Despite ongoing progress, important uncertainties remain, particularly regarding whether interventions should be applied universally or personalized based on a patient''s baseline microbiome composition. Reflecting these, recent trials have adopted stratification strategies, including those guided by baseline Akkermansia abundance. 57 Although clinical application is still evolving, microbiome modulation continues to emerge as a promising supportive strategy in oncology. Future efforts must focus on refining intervention design, understanding mechanisms of resistance, evaluating the durability of engraftment, and addressing barriers to support broader clinical integration. Regina Barragan-Carrillo Honoraria: Pfizer, Ipsen Travel, Accommodations, Expenses: Bayer Schering Pharma Sumanta K. Pal Speakers'' Bureau: MJH Life Sciences, IntrinsiQ, PeerView Research Funding: Exelixis (Inst), Merck (Inst), Osel (Inst), Genentech (Inst), CRISPR therapeutics (Inst), Adicet Bio (Inst), ArsenalBio (Inst), Xencor (Inst), MIYARISAN Pharmaceutical (Inst) Travel, Accommodations, Expenses: CRISPR therapeutics, Ipsen, Exelixis Open Payments Link: https://openpaymentsdata.cms.gov/physician/259259 No other potential conflicts of interest were reported. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Conception and design: Sumanta K. Pal Collection and assembly of data: Regina Barragan-Carrillo, Suma