 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Chang L., Cui Z., Shi D., et al., “Polyclonal Evolution of Fanconi Anemia to MDS and AML Revealed at Single Cell Resolution,” Experimental Hematology & Oncology 11, no. 1 (2022): 1–14. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Weeks L. D. and Ebert B. L., “Causes and Consequences of Clonal Hematopoiesis,” Blood 142, no. 26 (2023): 2235–2246. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Richards S., Aziz N., Bale S., et al., “Standards and Guidelines for the Interpretation of Sequence Variants: A Joint Consensus Recommendation of the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics and the Association for Molecular Pathology,” Genetics in Medicine 17, no. 5 (2015): 405–424. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Reinig E. F., Rubinstein J. D., Patil A. T., et al., “Needle in a Haystack or Elephant in the Room? Identifying Germline Predisposition Syndromes in the Setting of a New Myeloid Malignancy Diagnosis,” Leukemia 37, no. 8 (2023): 1589–1599. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Data S1. CNR2-7-e2141-s002.pdf (1.1MB, pdf) Table S1. CNR2-7-e2141-s001.docx (21.8KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',259,'2025-10-03 14:14:44','2025-10-03 14:14:44');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(494,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11688255','Case study of a neuroendocrine tumor of uncertain origin: single-cell transcriptomics unravels potential primary location.','Tatyana F, Avsievich E, Salimgereeva D, Antysheva Z, Maluchenko A, Maksimov D, Feidorov I, Voloshin M, Glazova O, Bodunova N, Karnaukhov N, Volchkov P, Krupinova J.','2024','histogenesis taal dasari waisberg ectopia chetty weinred okasha fortuitously waisberg papilla milione gastrin gastrinoma atema microenvironmental lipase amylase esophagogastroduodenoscopy antero bulboduodenal multispiral msct endosonographic loginov msct posteromedial postbulbar gastroduodenal pedicle endosonographic endosonographic multinodular histochemical gastrin adrenocorticotropic ca19 loginov pedicle amylase monotonous microvesicular pt2 l0v0 pn0 haematoxylin x400 x400 x400 x400 annotate numbat mistakenly compositional compositional sinet pannet pannet chr6 161785820g prkn enst00000366898 rs34424986 mitophagy prkn scarpa pommier scarpa missenses sift polyphen coactivators psip1 nr2f1 erap2 psip1 psip1 ternary psip1 kmt2a menin demoen weihua sinets pommier cna pannet sinet sinet pommier cna oncoplot cna contradict pannet peculiarities dasari folkestad folkestad msct endosonographic taei taei latters nosological dehal histogenesis tatyana frolova ekaterina avsievich salimgereeva volchkov krupinova volchkov krupinova tatyana frolova ekaterina avsievich salimgereeva zoia antysheva alesia maluchenko maksimov ilia feidorov voloshin olga glazova tatyana frolova ekaterina avsievich salimgereeva krupinova salimgereeva ilia feidorov voloshin nikolay karnaukhov ilia feidorov nikolay karnaukhov natalia bodunova volchkov krupinova tatyana frolova ekaterina avsievich salimgereeva alesia maluchenko zoia antysheva voloshin nikolay karnaukhov tatyana frolova ekaterina avsievich salimgereeva zoia antysheva alesia maluchenko olga glazova natalia bodunova nikolay karnaukhov volchkov krupinova gse282219 wjgo v12 grunn aburi rieckhof komissarova bodei clemons s41588 atema amri rauws gouma nieveen gastrinomas barkas petukhov kharchenko steiger rydbirk biederstedt pagoda2 chetty weinred heterotopic dasari dehal walbolt retroperitoneum tpp demoen matthijssens reunes palhais lintermans fijalkowski taminau akele psip1 ledgf eado sciadv ado6765 slowikowski s12276 folkestad wasmuth mjønes fougner hauso fossmark cancers13163985 kiezun pedamallu banck kanwar karpathakis cdkn1b soldatov kurkiewicz biederstedt kharchenko s41587 dowrey villacorta titchen johri dellostritto mackichan inga intertumoral eadd9668 sciadv add9668 insulinomas edil klimstra maitra schulick choti daxx ohara okamura miyasaka yamabuki komuro suzuoki iwashiro s40792 waqar mohiuddin ehtesham jalees ayub abbottabad krug scharf ellenrieder michl homayunfar e0188876 blenkiron robb yeong s41525 pommier milione parente grillo zamboni mastracci capella fassan vanoli ampullary jejunum pathologica 951x okasha bassiouni ela gemeie ezzat cheong v16 i36 scarpa nones corbo lawlor mafficini nature21063 taal 8_3 taei tha thinayyan radcr omote okazaki yanai yoshino heterotopic s12328 waisberg joppert netto vasconcellos sartini s0004 rau diebold opoku boateng ramcharan boudreaux woltering e24635 weihua tsan fidler mitophagy dagohoy mares abdalla vauthey somervaille rozenblatt meyerson menin tethers ledgf mll leukemogenesis v112 gse282219','Abstract Purpose Determining the primary origin of non-organ-confined neuroendocrine tumors (NETs) for accurate diagnosis and management. Neuroendocrine tumors are rare neoplasms with diverse clinical behaviors. Determining their primary origin remains challenging in cases of non-organ-confined NETs. This study explores the histogenesis of a retroperitoneal, non-functional NET localized between the duodenum and pancreatic head, utilizing advanced molecular diagnostics to elucidate its probable primary source. Methods Initial diagnostic methods, including imaging and histopathology, failed to resolve the tumor’s origin. The tumor was subjected to single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and whole exome sequencing (WES). Publicly available transcriptomic datasets from pancreatic and small intestine NETs were used to develop and validate a molecular gene signature for tissue-of-origin identification. Results The gene signature distinguished pancreatic and small intestine NETs with high accuracy. The tumor cells presented a molecular profile consistent with a pancreatic origin, likely derived from ectopic pancreatic tissue. Conclusions This case demonstrates the value of integrating scRNA-seq and WES for the molecular characterization of complex NETs. Identifying the tumor’s pancreatic origin informed a targeted management approach, avoiding unnecessary systemic treatment and underscoring the potential of single-cell approaches in personalized oncology. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s00432-024-06071-z. Keywords: Neuroendocrine tumor, Duodenal NET, Pancreatic NET, Non-organ-confined NET, Ectopic pancreatic tissue, Single-cell RNA‐seq Introduction Neuroendocrine tumors (NETs) are well-differentiated neoplasms originating from neuroendocrine cells. The incidence of NETs varies from 1 to 5 cases per 100,000 patients, and NETs account for less than 0.5% of all malignant neoplasms (Taal and Visser 2004 ; Yao et al. 2008 ). In the past 30 years, due to the development of new diagnostic methods, there has been an increase in NET morbidity, irrespective of localization. At present, gastrointestinal NETs are considered to be the most common (Dasari et al. 2017 ). An ectopic pancreas refers to abnormally localized pancreatic tissue not connected with the main pancreas that has its own vascular and ductal systems (Waisberg et al. 2013 ). Although ectopic pancreas represents the most frequently occurring type of ectopia in the abdominal cavity, with an incidence of 0.5 to 13% in the general population at autopsy (Ryu et al. 2010 ), NETs deriving from ectopic pancreatic tissue are exceedingly rare. Less than 10 clinical cases of NETs in aberrant pancreatic lobes, including those in the stomach and retroperitoneal tissue, have been described in the literature (Chetty and Weinred 2004 ; Okasha et al. 2013 ; Tanaka et al. 2018 ). Additionally, ectopic pancreatic tissue in the duodenum is observed in 25–35% of patients with ectopic pancreatic tissue (Kaneko et al. 2019 ), yet, to our knowledge, no cases of NET derivation from ectopic tissue of this localization have been described so far. The very low number of published case reports precludes any general conclusions regarding the prognosis for NETs deriving from ectopic pancreatic tissue. It may only be assumed that the prognosis for NETs deriving from ectopic tissue would be similar to that of NETs originating from the pancreas. Assuming the secondary nature of this tumor, it is imperative to consider the possibility of a duodenal origin of the NET. This type of NET may remain undiagnosed for an extended period of time, is frequently identified fortuitously, as they often manifest without specific clinical symptoms (Waisberg et al. 2013 ). Duodenal NETs account for 2–4% of all gastroenteropancreatic neoplasms (Wang et al. 2021 ). Notably, 40–60% of these tumors have metastases in the regional lymph nodes (Ahmed 2020 ). The available data suggest that the prognosis in duodenal NETs is uncertain (Koenig et al. 2017 ). It should be noted that NETs of the duodenal bulb and papilla constitute a separate group as these are more consistent with gastric NETs than duodenal tumors in terms of their clinical and morphological characteristics (Milione 2021 ). To our knowledge, the reports on primary non-organ-confined NETs are very rare. It is, however, well established that approximately 13% of all neuroendocrine neoplasms are considered to be “of unknown primary origin” (Yao et al. 2008 ). Meanwhile, primary non-organ-confined tumors may remain unidentified due to extensive lymphatic involvement (Khan et al. 2015 ). Nevertheless, it is estimated that 90% of neuroendocrine neoplasms of unknown origin are neuroendocrine carcinomas (NECs), which are poorly differentiated tumors (Wang et al. 2016 ). Also, such neoplasms are frequently regarded as primary tumors localizing in lymph nodes (for example gastrin producing NETs on so-called “gastrinoma triangle”) (Atema et al. 2012 ). In cases where the nature of the neoplasm is not readily apparent, the application of molecular diagnostics is essential. Although individual markers can be used for this purpose, they may not be sufficient given the inter-patient heterogeneity in patients with NETs of the same localization. Bulk RNA sequencing enables a more comprehensive characterization of the transcriptional profile of each tumor sample and the establishment of the gene signatures for the identification of the primary tumors based on the results of differential gene expression analysis. On the other hand, a tumor sample generally includes microenvironmental components rather than cancer cells alone, and the expression profile of the sample represents an average of heterogeneous cell types. Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) provides a higher resolution of the tumor cell populations and expression patterns. This approach enables the exploration of the expression profile of each cell and the distinction between cell types within the sample, for example, neuroendocrine, immune, vascular, endothelial cell populations, etc. (Fan et al. 2020 ). In addition, copy number variation (CNV) analysis may be conducted based on scRNA-seq data. This analysis allows to distinguish the malignant cells from the adjacent normal cells with more precision. Such joint exploration of cell’s expression and CNV profiles provides unique ability for deep tumor tissue profiling. In this study, we report a clinical case of a patient with a non-functional non-organ-confined neuroendocrine tumor localized between the duodenal wall and the head of the pancreas. We also performed scRNA-seq and whole exome sequencing (WES) for molecular diagnostics. Using publicly available bulk RNA-seq and scRNA-seq data from pancreatic and small intestinal NETs, we discovered that the pancreas was the most probable organ of origin in the case of this NET. The use of scRNA-seq in this case allowed us to assess expression patterns specifically in the malignant cells, while excluding the related neuroendocrine, immune, endothelial and mesenchymal microenvironment. Case presentation Patient A., female, aged 37, was admitted to the emergency room with acute abdominal pain in September 2023. Acute pancreatitis was initially suspected, however blood serum analysis did not show elevated lipase and amylase levels. Esophagogastroduodenoscopy revealed antero-medial external compression of the bulboduodenal junction. Abdominal multispiral computed tomography (MSCT) with intravenous contrast was performed, which did not confirm pancreatic disease. Unfortunately, no images were available for further analysis. Based on the results of a comprehensive medical evaluation, surgical conditions were ruled out, and the patient was discharged from the regional urgent care clinic. A scheduled endosonographic examination with fine-needle biopsy was performed in October 2023 for the clarification of the diagnosis, which revealed a G1 neuroendocrine neoplasm. The patient was then referred to the Competence Center for neuroendocrine tumor management of the Loginov Moscow Scientific Center. Due to the discordance of the previously obtained data, on December 20th, 2023 the patient underwent additional MSCT with intravenous contrast enhancement of chest, abdomen, and pelvis. In the posteromedial wall of the postbulbar part of the duodenum a neoplasm 20 × 37 × 39 mm was visualised, with rounded shape, both cystic and solid components, showing active contrast enhancement in the arterial phase. The tumor was also adjacent to the gastroduodenal artery and the inferior vena cava. Its cranial part was in contact with the head of the pancreas (6 mm), closer to the posterior surface (Fig 1 a-c). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab (a - c) Computed tomography scans with intravenous contrast enhancement. Tumor size measures are illustrated in the images (in cm). Arrows indicate: 1 - tumor; 2 – duodenal lumen; 3 – biopsy needle; 4 – pancreatic tissue. (d) Intraoperative view. Arrows indicate: 1 – tumor; 2 – pancreatic tissue adjacent to the tumor; 3 – vascular pedicle of the tumor; 4 – posterior surface of the descending part of the duodenum. (e) Endosonographic data. Arrows indicate: 1 - tumor; 2 – duodenal lumen; 3 – biopsy needle; 4 – pancreatic tissue This localization was also confirmed during a second endosonographic examination conducted on December 22nd, 2023: in the projection of the descending part of the duodenum between the wall of the duodenum and the external contour of the pancreas, a multinodular neoplasm of medium echogenicity, with smooth contours and a heterogeneous structure, clearly demarcated from the surrounding structures was visualized, measuring 38 × 25 mm (Fig. 1 e). However, it was impossible to draw any conclusions regarding its organ of origin. Due to the large size of the tumor it was difficult to judge the echo layer the tumor originated from. A fine needle biopsy with consequent histochemical testing was conducted. A well-differentiated G1 neuroendocrine tumor with a proliferative hotspot index activity (Ki-67) up to 2% and a positive status for somatostatin type 2 receptors (3+) was diagnosed. Hormonal activity of the tumor was ruled out based on laboratory test results, with no evidence of elevated NET biomarker levels (Chromogranin A, serotonin, gastrin, calcitonin, parathyroid and adrenocorticotropic hormone). Tumor marker testing revealed only a slight increase in CA19-9 levels to 31 U/mL (< 27 U/ml). In March 2024, the patient underwent surgical treatment, namely laparoscopic tumor enucleation at the Loginov Moscow Clinical Scientific Center. During the intervention, the descending portion of the duodenum was mobilized, and a tumor with no signs of invasive growth in a fibrous capsule was visualized. The neoplasm was adjacent to the pancreatic tissue along an area of approximately 5 mm and had its own vascular pedicle (Fig. 1 d). The postoperative period was unremarkable, and the drainage was removed 4 days following the surgery; there was no evidence of amylase activity in the drain fluid. The patient was discharged 7 days following the surgery and experienced no complications. In the surgically removed tumor, the histology was represented by a solid-trabecular architecture of monotonous tumor cells with round nuclei, microvesicular “salt and pepper” chromatin and moderately eosinophilic granular cytoplasm (Fig. 2 a). Immunohistochemistry showed a positive diffuse cytoplasmic reaction of the tumor with antibodies to chromogranin A and synaptophysin (brown staining with DAB), (Fig. 2 b and c). The marker of proliferative activity Ki-67 was stained in “hot spots” up to 12% of tumor cell nuclei (Fig. 2 d), which was higher compared to the result of fine-needle aspiration biopsy due to the heterogeneity of the tumor. Histopathological diagnosis: well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas, grade 2, pT2 L0V0 Pn0 R0. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab (a) Histological examination of the surgical specimen of the pancreas (haematoxylin-eosin) of patient A. (x400). (b) Immunohistochemical examination of the surgical specimen of the pancreas of patient A, chromogranin A staining (x400). (c) Immunohistochemical examination of the surgical specimen of the pancreas of patient A, synaptophysin staining (x400). (d) Immunohistochemical examination of the surgical specimen of the pancreas of patient A, ki67 staining (x400) Due to the difficulties in diagnosis, we aimed to utilize scRNA-seq to elucidate the potential tumor tissue of origin. Fresh postoperative tumor tissue was used for the cell suspension and 10X Genomics 5’ library preparation. After data preprocessing and quality control 3092 high-quality cells remained. Annotation of main cell types was conducted using differential expression analysis and cell type marker databases. As a result, neuroendocrine tumor cells, fibroblasts, smooth muscle cells, endothelial cells, and different types of lymphoid and myeloid immune cells were identified (Fig. 3 a, b). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab ScRNA-seq analysis of the tumor sample. (a) Annotated cells from the tumor sample on UMAP coordinates. (b) Dot plot with cell type markers used to annotate cell clusters. (c) Tumor cells on UMAP coordinates, black - normal neuroendocrine cells without CNV, light blue - tumor cells from clone 1, blue - tumor cells from clone 2. (d) Plot with CNV patterns in the two identified clones (above), scheme of clonal evolution from normal cells to clone 1 and clone 2 (below) CNV inferring and clonal dynamics analysis using Numbat ( Gao et al. 2023 ) revealed two CNV clones with different genotypes in the tumor component (Fig. 3 c, d). Only 10 microenvironment cells were mistakenly assigned to the tumor, which confirmed high precision in CNV analysis of the tumor sample (Fig. S1 a). Cells from clone 1 were characterized by deletions in chromosomes 2, 3, and 9, and the remaining cells could represent more recent clone, developed from the former through acquiring deletion in chromosome 10 (Fig. 3 d). Top differentially expressed genes between two clones are shown in Fig. S1 b. Due to the rarity of gastroenteropancreatic NETs, there is currently a lack of molecular data on these tumors. To the best of our knowledge there is currently only one study with scRNA-seq data on NETs of small intestine and pancreatic origin (Hoffman et al. 2023 ). Using scRNA-seq data from this study, we first characterized and compared compositional patterns of different samples (Fig. S1 c-e). We observed strong compositional differences between samples from the two datasets (Fig. 4 a). Cells of the immune component were better represented in samples from the Hoffman et al. study due to enrichment for CD45-positive cells in one of the two samples from each patient. In contrast, stromal cells, including fibroblasts, smooth muscle cells, and endothelial cells were almost exclusively found in our tumor sample. It is noteworthy that microenvironment cells comprised a significant share (85%) of our tumor sample, highlighting the importance of the single-cell approach in determining tumor cells’ origin. Notably, the large differences in the microenvironment may be due to the fundamentally different localization of the tumor. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Comparison to published data and tumor origin identification. (a) Bar plot with cellular compositions of different tumor samples; pNET and siNET - pancreatic and small intestine NET samples from published data, respectively, tumor - NET sample from this study. (b) Brief scheme of score construction to identify tumor origin. (c) Box plots with the score, colors represent samples, box plot represent distribution of score either for tumor cells, or for microenvironment cells from different samples Next, we utilized bulk gene expression data from the study conducted by Alvarez et al. to establish the signature of genes that can distinguish pancreatic and small intestine NETs (Fig. 4 b) (Alvarez et al. 2018 ). After differential expression analysis and feature selection with a machine learning classifier 100 genes remained, among which 39 were specific to pancreatic NETs and 61 were specifically expressed in NETs from the small intestine (Table S1 ). These genes were used to calculate a gene signature of NETs formulated as a total activity of PanNET-specific genes minus total activity of small intestine NETs-specific genes. The gene signature was validated by assigning the score to cells from 4 pancreatic and 3 small intestine samples from the abovementioned scRNA-seq dataset (Fig. 4 c) (Hoffman et al. 2023 ). Based on clustering and cell type annotation cells from each tumor sample were divided into tumor neuroendocrine cells and cells of microenvironment. Tumor cells from our sample were those annotated as neuroendocrine cells and possessing CNV alterations. The average score appeared to be about zero in microenvironment cells, above zero – in pancreatic tumor cells, and below zero – in small intestine tumor cells. Tumor cells from our sample demonstrated positive score and, therefore, likely to represent the pancreatic primary tumor. To assess tumor of origin genomic features and PanNET biomarkers, we conducted whole exome sequencing (WES) of tumor material and the patient’s blood. Germline variant analysis yielded only one variant of interest, a pathogenic heterozygous variant chr6:161785820G > A in the gene PRKN (ENST00000366898.6, c.823 C > T, rs34424986). Mitophagy aberrations, which are caused by variants in PRKN , are suggested to be connected with neoplastic transformations (Xie et al. 2021 ). However, no connection with any NET type has been detected yet. Somatic mutation analysis yielded a low tumor mutation burden of 0.45 Mutation/Mb, which is characteristic for both pancreatic and small intestine NETs (Francis et al. 2013 ; Scarpa et al. 2017 ). None of the genes described as frequently mutated in each of these tumors of origin (Francis et al. 2013 ; Lawrence et al. 2018 ; Lim and Pommier 2021 ; Scarpa et al. 2017 ) were mutated in this tumor, and no clinically relevant fusions were discovered. However, the tumor possessed somatic mutations (missenses predicted pathogenic by SIFT and PolyPhen) with high variant allele frequency in transcriptional coactivators PSIP1 and NR2F1 , and peptidase ERAP2 . (Fig. 7a). The importance of PSIP1 mutation was evidenced by loss of heterozygosity, which left the tumor with the mutated copy only. PSIP1 is a critical component of the ternary PSIP1-KMT2A-Menin complex, and its impairment could be at least partially equivalent to disrupting MEN1 (Demoen et al. 2024 ; Weihua et al. 2008 ; Yokoyama et al. 2008 ). MEN1 loss of function is a frequent event in NETs, although both in PanNETs and siNETs (Jiao et al. 2011 ; Lawrence et al. 2018 ; Lim and Pommier 2021 ). Tumor CNA profile demonstrated low number of alterations, mostly losses of heterozygosity in chromosomes 2, 3, and 9 (Fig. 5 b). This profile is more in line with PanNET profiles, where LOH events and aneuploidy are more common features (Hong et al. 2020 ; Lawrence et al. 2018 ), while lacking characteristic siNET’s copy number gains and deletion on chromosome 18, the latter present in more than 50% of siNET cases. (Francis et al. 2013 ; Lim and Pommier 2021 ). Also, notably, the tumor CNA profile from the exome lacked high confidence deletion of chromosome 10, which was defining for clone 2 in single cell profiling. WES and scRNA-seq were derived from different, albeit spatially close, tumor slices; such difference demonstrates that even non-aggressive NETs might possess notable spatial heterogeneity. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Results of WES data analysis. (a) Germline and somatic mutation oncoplot. (b) Somatic CNA profile Overall, tumor WES did not yield any features that would be sufficient to make a decision about tumor of origin on its own. It may be not an unusual occurrence for NETs, as tumor genomic features are sparse data and even more sparse in less aggressive tumor types as NET. Still, it did not contradict scRNA-seq prediction and even provided some evidence supporting classifying this tumor as PanNET. Discussion In this study, we report a rare case of a neuroendocrine neoplasm which is likely derived from the ectopic tissue of the pancreas. The determination of its origin assisted in the evaluation of the clinical prognosis and further management of the patient’s condition. Based on the anatomical peculiarities of its localization, it was deemed possible that this tumor originated from ectopic pancreatic tissue. Given that the tumor had a fibrous capsule, was not attached to other organs and had its own blood supply system, a congenital condition (aberrant pancreatic tissue) was suspected. As previously mentioned, NETs originating from ectopic pancreatic tissue are very rare, hence it is not possible to assess the prognosis with sufficient accuracy. However, the prognosis may be comparable with that of pancreatic NETs. According to available data for 2000–2012 the 3-year overall survival rate in patients with PanNETs was 62%, and 5-year was 50% (Dasari et al. 2017 ). On the other hand, the tumor may have been a duodenal metastasis. In this case, both the prognosis and the treatment strategy would have been different. Data from a study by Folkestad et al. suggest that the 5-year overall survival rate in patients with duodenal NETs is 81.3% ( 2021 ). The authors also highlighted that NET size above 10 mm was associated with metastases to both regional and remote lymph nodes (Folkestad et al. 2021 ). However, in our case the primary tumor could not be visualized neither in MSCT scans nor during an endosonographic examination, which brought this hypothesis under question. On the other hand, these tests could not enable the exclusion of a small tumor of the duodenum or the small intestine. The possibility of the existence of primary non-organ-confined retroperitoneal tumors could not be ruled out. A number of publications reported similar neoplasms, although a detailed examination showed these to be in fact neuroendocrine cancers. For example, in 2020 Taei et al. reported a primary retroperitoneal non-organ-confined small-cell carcinoma that had been described as a neuroendocrine tumor (Taei et al. 2020 ), and Hong et al. described a tumor behind the ascending colon which was not associated with the latters’ anatomical structure ( 2012 ). However, in line with the current classification, these tumors fall into a different nosological group of neuroendocrine cancers. Still, there are publications that likely reported on truly retroperitoneal NETs. Dehal et al. described a G2 NET without a specific organ origin that was adjacent to the left kidney and the ureter ( 2015 ). It is likely that primary non-organ-confined retroperitoneal tumors may develop from ectopic neuroendocrine cells. However, retroperitoneal tumor localization can not serve as a guidance to the physician in terms of determining its origin and therefore further management. To improve our understanding of tumor origin, bulk or single-cell RNA sequencing can be applied. We developed and validated a gene signature based on the published RNA-seq data, which allowed us to differentiate between the pancreatic and intestinal origin of the NETs. The implementation of the developed gene signature for the analysis of the NET described within our study allowed us to identify the pancreatic tissue as the most probable source of the NET. Unlike bulk, single cell RNA-sequencing, additionally supported by WES data for CNV analysis, guaranteed tumor classification free of contamination with non-tumor tissue. Meanwhile, WES analysis played only a supplementary role in determining tissue origin. Given the low mutation load of NETs, it is not surprising that the tumor lacked any definitive genomic features. While WES analysis can be of more use in determining origin of tumors with more frequent genomic events, this highlights the potential offered by scRNA-seq. Therefore, bulk and single-cell RNA sequencing can guide the identification of tumor histogenesis, whenever standard testing is not helpful in addressing this question. In our case, this method helped to identify the origin of the tumor, the ectopic pancreatic neuroendocrine cells, which allowed us to exclude secondary lymph node involvement when considered together with morphological findings. Single-cell RNA-seq data obtained within our study will be published and freely available at GEO and may be used in future research. To our knowledge, this is the first time data of ectopic pancreatic NET sequenced with scRNA-seq or WES has been published. In light of the obtained data, the patient was offered active monitoring, and the clarification of the diagnosis helped to avoid the initiation of systemic pharmacological treatment. This makes molecular tumor profiling a promising method in tumor diagnostics. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (757.6KB, docx) Acknowledgements This research was funded by a grant from the Moscow government (research project No.1702-13/23) and was supported by the Ministry of Science and Higher Education of the Russian Federation (agreement # No.075-03-2022-107/10). Author contributions Conceptualization was performed by [Tatyana Frolova], [Ekaterina Avsievich], [Diana Salimgereeva], [Pavel Volchkov] and [Julia Krupinova]. [Pavel Volchkov] and [Julia Krupinova] contributed to funding acquisition. Investigation was performed by [Tatyana Frolova], [Ekaterina Avsievich], [Diana Salimgereeva], [Zoia Antysheva], [Alesia Maluchenko], [Denis Maksimov], [Ilia Feidorov] and [Mark Voloshin], [Olga Glazova]. Methodology was conducted by [Tatyana Frolova], [Ekaterina Avsievich], [Diana Salimgereeva]. [Julia Krupinova] contributed to project administration. Resources were performed by [Diana Salimgereeva], [Ilia Feidorov], [Mark Voloshin], [Nikolay Karnaukhov]. Supervision was conducted by [Ilia Feidorov], [Nikolay Karnaukhov], [Natalia Bodunova], [Pavel Volchkov] and [Julia Krupinova]. [Tatyana Frolova], [Ekaterina Avsievich], [Diana Salimgereeva], [Alesia Maluchenko], [Zoia Antysheva], [Mark Voloshin] and [Nikolay Karnaukhov] contributed to visualization. Writing - original draft was performed by [Tatyana Frolova], [Ekaterina Avsievich], [Diana Salimgereeva], [Zoia Antysheva] and 