mpact of exogenous galectin-9 on human T cells. J. Biol. Chem. 2015;290:16797–16811. doi: 10.1074/jbc.M115.661272. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 175. Peng F., Huang Y., Li M.Y., Li G.Q., Huang H.C., Guan R., Chen Z.C., Liang S.P., Chen Y.H. Dissecting characteristics and dynamics of differentially expressed proteins during multistage carcinogenesis of human colorectal cancer. World J. Gastroenterol. 2016;22:4515–4528. doi: 10.3748/wjg.v22.i18.4515. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 176. Xue H., Yang R.-Y., Tai G., Liu F.-T. Galectin-12 inhibits granulocytic differentiation of human NB4 promyelocytic leukemia cells while promoting lipogenesis. J. Leukoc. Biol. 2016;100:657–664. doi: 10.1189/jlb.1HI0316-134R. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',150,'2025-10-03 14:16:46','2025-10-03 14:16:46');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(504,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12381419','Primary neuroendocrine tumors of the breast according to the WHO 2019 classification; real life experiences of rare tumor types of the breast.','Sali S, Sali M, Caner B, Ocak B, Coban E, Coskun A, Ozsen M, Oyucu Orhan S, Sahin AB, Deligonul A, Cubukcu E, Tolunay S, Evrensel T.','2025','reevaluated neuroendocrin reevaluated feyrter ibcs ibc hypercellular nst ibc nst ibc ibc nst ibc ibc nst uludag reevaluated uludag waiving halton multinodular chek premenopause postmenopause til til taxane reexamination ibc nst ibc nst ibc nst carcinoembryonic ca19 premenopause postmenopause veya til til premenopause postmenopause veya til til nonmetastatic til til imagings nucleoli ibc nst ibc nst ibc nst stainings stainings granin ibc nst cdk cdk cdk monarch abemaciclib ibc nst reevaluated reevaluated oyaf171_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1 oyaf171_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1 ilker ercan uludag seda sali nilufer mursel sali uludag gorukle burcu caner ataturk birol ocak yuksek ihtisas yildirim eyup coban uludag gorukle alper coskun uludag gorukle ozsen uludag gorukle sibel oyucu orhan nilufer ahmet bilgehan uludag gorukle deligonul uludag gorukle erdem cubukcu uludag gorukle sahsine tolunay uludag gorukle turkkan evrensel uludag gorukle seda sali seda_ist1987 sedasali uludag feyrter mammae solidum gelatinosum mammae frankf rakha horsch anlauf weimar dasari e1318 e1329 jorns lavigne menet tille sapino righi cassoni apocrine rindi uccella uhlig dum gorbokon taupenot secretogranin nejmra021405 oki uchino cancers14010196 albarracin angarita shaughnessy abemaciclib preplanned monarche oyaf171_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1 oyaf171_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1 seda sali seda_ist1987 sedasali uludag','Abstract Background Neuroendocrine neoplasms (NEN) originate in many body parts, with 70% reported from the gastrointestinal system, 25% from the lungs. NENs of the breast account for < 1% of all NENs. The primary breast NEN nomenclature was changed in the most recent World Health Organization classification in 2019. Since the pathological classification has changed several times, there are no definitive data on treatment management. Methods In this study, patients followed up in our clinic were pathologically reevaluated according to the latest classification. Results The clinical characteristics, treatments, recurrence, and survival status of 36 patients with early stage primary breast NEN were examined. A statistically significant difference was determined in respect of overall survival (OS) for the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Score (ECOG PS), type of surgery, age ≥ 70 years, PR positivity, and tumor size. A low ECOG PS, younger age, breast-conserving surgery (BCS), and small tumor diameter were associated with better survival. A low ECOG PS, early stage, BCS, and fewer than 4 malignant lymph nodes were associated with better disease-free survival. All the patients in this study were at an early stage and were treated for typical breast cancer, although some patients diagnosed with neuroendocrine carcinoma (NEC) received cisplatin and etoposide treatment. Conclusions In our study, the prognosis of breast NEN patients was better than the literature data. Maybe the prognosis of patients evaluated as breast NEN according to the new classification is better than the older classification groups. Keywords: neuroendocrin tumors, synaptophysin, breast cancer, survival Implications for Practice. We aimed to gather information about the prognosis of primary breast neuroendocrine tumors (NET). In our study, we observed a higher survival rate compared to previously published data. This discrepancy may be due to the fact that patients with elevated marker levels and greater morphological similarity according to the new 2019 WHO classification were diagnosed with NET. Additionally, elevated marker levels may be linked to a better prognosis. The strength of the current study lies in the fact that all patients since 2010 have been reevaluated and re-diagnosed with NET or neuroendocrine carcinoma (NEC) according to the latest WHO classification. Introduction The neuroendocrine features of breast cancer (BC) were first identified by Feyrter and Hartmann in 1963, who described the morphological similarities between intestinal carcinoids and invasive BCs (IBCs). 1 The first case series was reported in 1977. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), neuroendocrine tumors (NETs) and neuroendocrine carcinomas (NECs) are rare subtypes of IBC. The estimated probability of occurrence is 1%. 2 NETs typically originate from gastrointestinal and pulmonary tissue, with gastrointestinal and pancreatic NENs accounting for 70% of all NENs and lung-originating NENs accounting for approximately 25%. 3 The classification of breast NETs has undergone changes in the WHO classification in 2003, 2012, and 2019. Neuroendocrine neoplasms (NENs) of BC were first recognized in the 3rd edition of the WHO classification in 2003. The most recent classification of primary NETs in the breast was changed in 2019 ( Table S1 ). 4 Primary breast NEN; Unlike the 2012 WHO classification, in 2019, it was defined as morphological neuroendocrine similarity and neuroendocrine expression being over 90%. In addition, solid papillary carcinoma and hypercellular mucinous carcinoma were excluded from this group in the latest classification. In the 5th edition of the WHO classification (2019), NENs are divided into 2 main groups: NETs and NECs. According to the 2019 WHO classification, breast NENs are categorized as NET if well differentiated and NEC if poorly differentiated. 5 Histological grade is evaluated using the Nottingham score. Unlike NENs of other organs, mitotic index, necrosis, and Ki-67 values have been shown to not have prognostic significance in primary NENs of the breast. The most important prognostic factors have been reported to be the stage of the disease and the Nottingham grade. 4 However, tumors with < 90% neuroendocrine cells are named no special type (NST) BC with neuroendocrine (NE) features. 4 Due to the limited number of trials and case series, the differences in prognosis between primary breast NETs and typical BCs have not been clearly demonstrated. 6 NETs generally have a better prognosis compared to carcinomas in other organ NENs. However, it cannot be concluded that NETs have a better prognosis in BC compared to IBC-NST. A study evaluating 1,372 BC patients, 52 of whom had NET, 172 had IBC with NE features, found no survival differences between IBC-NST and NET patients. In the study, IBC with NE features had worse disease-free survival (DFS), while NET patients had similar DFS to IBC-NST patients. Increased marker expression levels were associated with better prognosis. 7 The aim of this study was to examine the treatment response, prognosis, and clinical characteristics of patients diagnosed with breast NET according to this new classification. Methods Patients who received treatment for BC were documented at Bursa Uludağ University Hospital between January 2010 and June 2023 ( Figure 1 ). Since the new histopathological WHO classification was being used by 2019, the specimen preparations of all patients with diagnosed with BC between 2010 and 2023 and with synaptophysin or chromogranin staining were reevaluated by the pathology department and patients diagnosed with NEN according to the new classification were included in the study. The patient’s age, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Score (ECOG PS), and comorbidities were examined. The immunohistochemical staining characteristics of the tumor, type of operation, lymph node metastasis, treatments applied, and last follow-up dates were noted. Patients who had received neoadjuvant therapy were staged according to the breast carcinoma tumor, node, and metastasis (TNM) clinical prognostic stage groups (American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)/Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) 8th edition). Patients who had undergone surgery first were staged according to breast carcinoma TNM pathological prognostic stage groups (AJCC/UICC 8th edition). Early and locally advanced stage patients were identified and recurrence status was followed up. The treatment responses of patients who had received neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NACT) were evaluated using the Miller-Payne Grading System. The patient’s age, ECOG PS, and comorbidities were examined. The immunohistochemical staining characteristics of the tumor, type of operation, lymph node metastasis, treatments applied, and last follow-up dates were noted. A total of 45 female patients diagnosed according to the 5th edition of the BC WHO classification were evaluated. Finally, 38 patients for whom data and imaging data were available were included in the study. Two patients were in the metastatic stage and were excluded from the statistical analysis. Therefore, the final analysis comprised 36 patients ( Figure 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flow chart of patients included in the study. Overall survival (OS) was defined as the period from the time of diagnosis until death or until the data cutoff. Disease-free survival was defined as the time from diagnosis until the patient was diagnosed with invasive disease again or the data cutoff date. When the retrospective electronic records of the patients were examined, it was seen that visceral metastases were confirmed by biopsy in 2 of the 4 patients who had DFS. The other 2 patients who presented with bone metastases showed radiological progression during follow-up. This study was performed in accordance with the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the Clinical Research Ethics Committee of Bursa Uludağ University Faculty of Medicine (No. 2023-13/18) on June 13, 2023, waiving the need for informed consent due to the retrospective design of the study. Data obtained in the study were analyzed statistically using SPSS vn. 22.0 software. Conformity of the data to normal distribution was determined using the Shapiro–Wilk test. Descriptive statistics were stated as mean and standard deviation (mean ± SD) values if the data were normally distributed and as median (min-max) values if the data were not normally distributed. Categorical data were presented as number ( n ) and percentage (%). Comparisons between groups were performed using the Mann–Whitney U -test. Comparisons of categorical data between groups were performed using the chi-square test, Fisher’s exact chi-square test, and the Fisher-Freeman-Halton test. Survival analyses were performed using Kaplan–Meier analysis. Survival comparisons were performed using the log-rank test. Descriptive statistics of survival were presented as mean and standard error values. Relationships between variables were examined using Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient. In statistical analysis, the significance level was set at α = 0.05. Results The mean age of the patients was 59.22 ± 13.76 years. Twenty-four patients were HER-2 negative, estrogen, and progesterone receptor positive; 6 patients were HER-2 negative, estrogen positive, and progesterone negative; 3 patients were triple positive; 1 patient was HER-2 positive and hormone negative; 1 patient was HER-2 positive, estrogen positive, and progesterone negative; 1 patient was triple-negative ( Table S3 ). The proliferation index (Ki-67) values of the patients were examined, and the median was 17% (range: 1-70). The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Status (ECOG PS) of all the patients was ≤ 2. At least one comorbid disease was present in 25 patients, such as diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, coronary artery disease, cardiac arrhythmia, cerebrovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, asthma, chronic hepatitis, rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, and multinodular goiter. Metastasis of at least one lymph node was determined in 17 patients. There was no family history (first and second degree) of BC in 30 patients; it was present in 4 patients, and information on family history was not obtained in 2 patients. Genetic mutation results of 28 patients could not be found in the files. Eight patients’ germline genetic mutation results were found; 5 patients had no mutation, 2 patients were breast cancer susceptibility gene-1 (BRCA-1) mutants, and 1 patient had a Checkpoint Kinase-2 (CHEK-2) gene mutation. Because the number of patients with known germline mutation status was insufficient, comparisons could not be made in these patients. Therefore, patients with immunohistochemical BRCA expression loss are indicated in the table. Both synaptophysin and chromogranin staining were positive in most of the patients (n = 22; 61.1%); synaptophysin was diffuse positive in 12 (33.3%) patients and chromogranin in 2 (5.6%) patients. The clinical and pathological characteristics and family history of the patients are summarized in Table 1 . Table 1. The clinical and pathological patient characteristics. Patient characteristics Type N = 36 (%) Age (years) <70 25 (69.4) ≥70 11 (30.6) Diagnosis NET (31) 31 (86.1) NEC (5) 5 (13.9) Menopausal status Premenopause 8 (22.2) Postmenopause 28 (77.8) Tumor location Right 17 (47.2) Left 19 (57.8) ECOG PS 0 24 (66.7) 1 8 (22.2) 2 4 (11.1) Stage 1 18 (50) 2 11 (30.6) 3 7 (19.4) Tumor size (cm) <2 12 (33.3) 2 ≤ and < 5 19 (52.8) 5≤ 5 (13.9) Grade 1 2 (5.6) 2 17 (47.2) 3 15 (41.7) Unknown 2 (5.6) ER status Positive 34 (94.4) Negative 2 (5.6) PR status Positive 27 (75) Negative 9 (25) HER-2 status Positive 5 (13.9) Negative 31 (86.1) Kİ-67 (%) <20 10 (27.8) ≥20 17 (47.2) TIL ≤5 25 (69.4) >5 8 (22.2) Unknown 3 (8.3) Evaluated lymph nodes <4 16 (44.4) ≥4 and ≤ 10 14 (38.9) >10 6 (16.7) Malignant lymph node 0 16 (44.4) ≥1 and ≤ 4 11 (30.6) >4 6 (16.7) Unknown 3 (8.3) Family history Present 4 (11.1) Absent 30 (83.3) Unknown 2 (5.6) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: BRCA, breast cancer gene; ECOG PS, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Status; ER, estrogen receptor; HER-2, epidermal growth factor receptor 2; IHC, immunohistochemistry; Ki-67, proliferation index; PR, progesterone receptor; TIL, tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. The clinical characteristics of the patients were examined and 5 patients were diagnosed with NEC and 31 patients were diagnosed with NET. Nine of these patients underwent NACT. Four of these 9 patients who received neoadjuvant chemotherapy had NEC and 5 had NET. One of the 4 patients with NEC received etoposide and platinum therapy, while the other 3 patients were treated with anthracycline-containing chemotherapy regimens. Three of the 5 patients with NET were treated with anthracycline-containing chemotherapy regimens, one with paclitaxel alone, and one with platinum, taxane, and anti-HER-2 therapy regimens because of HER-2 positive disease ( Table S3 ). Adjuvant chemotherapy was applied to 19 patients, of whom 16 were treated as classic BC. Three of the patients diagnosed with NEC during the postoperative period were treated with a combination of platinum and etoposide. The patients’ diagnoses were revised after the operation by reexamination of the postoperative pathology. These 3 patients had received anthracycline-containing treatment during the neoadjuvant period and had poor NACT response levels. Two of these 3 NEC patients subsequently developed metastasis to recurrence. One patient presented with cranial metastasis and the other had liver metastasis. These patients were histologically classified as luminal B-type, were treated with IBC-NST and continued to receive treatment at the end of this study. One patient with NEC diagnosis was treated as IBC-NST in the adjuvant setting, and remains alive at the time of writing. Other 2 patients with recurrences were diagnosed with NET. These patients also had adrenal and bone metastases, which were luminal A and B and were treated as IBC-NST. These 2 patients died. With the exception of 2 patients, all hormone-positive patients received adjuvant hormonal therapy. One patient could not receive hormonal treatment because of side effects, and the other died in the early postoperative period due to postoperative comorbid conditions (chronic renal failure, hypertension, diabetes) and surgical complications. A family history of BC was not present in 30 patients, was present in 4, and information of family history was not obtained in 2 patients. Tumor markers of 28 patients were evaluated at diagnosis, and data for the other 8 patients were not available. While only cancer antigen (CA) 15-3 was high in one of the 28 patients evaluated, both CA 15-3 and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) values were high in 2 patients. Three patients had only high CEA levels and 1 patient had only high CA19-9 levels. The tumor marker levels in the 21 patients evaluated were normal. Two of the 5 patients with elevated CEA levels at diagnosis progressed and died. In one patient who progressed, the marker level at diagnosis was unknown. The serum marker levels were normal in patients with other progression or death. When the study was terminated in June 2023, disease recurrence was observed in 4 patients, 2 of whom died. Two of the 4 patients with progression died, and their survival times were 60.4 months and 66.8 months. The survival times of the other deceased patients were 34.8 months and 4.1 months. Mortality was recorded for 4 patients in the entire group. Overall survival (OS) and DFS were evaluated in all 36 patients. The median follow-up time was 46.55 (min. 4.1-max. 150.4) months. At the end of follow-up, median OS and DFS could not be reached, but the estimated mean OS was 131.47 ± 8.74 months and DFS was 130.94 ± 8.93 months ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Overall survival (Chart 1) and DFS (Chart 2). There was a statistical difference in OS according to ECOG PS, type of surgery, age ≥ 70 years, PR positivity, and tumor size. Patients with ECOG PS ≤ 1 had longer OS than those with ECOG PS 2 ( P < .001). There was a statistically significant difference in the OS according to the type of surgery ( P = .037). The OS of patients who underwent total mastectomy was 109.68 ± 16.07 months, no patients died in cases with breast-conserving surgery (BCS), and the follow-up period varied between 18.6 and 148.8 months. The OS of patients aged < 70 years was longer than that of patients aged ≥ 70 years ( P = .029). PR positivity ( P = .032) and PR levels > 20% ( P = .036) contributed to OS. There was a statistical difference in OS between patients with tumor size < 2 cm and those with a tumor size ≥ 5 cm ( P = .016). There were no cases of death in patients with a tumor size < 2 cm, and the follow-up periods varied between 35.7 and 150.4 months. The average OS in patients with tumor size ≥ 5 cm was 61.34 ± 15.43 months. No statistically significant differences were found in OS in respect of other variables ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Comparison of overall survival times. n Mean ± SD P -value Pairwise comparisons P -value Age (years) <70 25 143.10 ± 5.55 .029 – ≥70 11 97.64 ± 21.67 Menopausal status Premenopause 8 132.51 ± 15.07 .977 – Postmenopause 28 129.95 ± 10.76 Comorbid disease No 11 136.13 ± 11.94 .735 – Yes 25 127.88 ± 11.69 Number of comorbid disease ≤1 15 140.03 ± 8.45 .321 – ≥2 21 119.81 ± 15.21 ECOG PS ≤1 32 145.77 ± 4.53 <.001 – 2 4 48.72 ± 15.91 Tumor location Right 17 126.63 ± 12.37 .484 – Left 19 105.84 ± 8.73 ER (%) <90 12 139.95 ± 9.77 .395 – ≥ 90 24 124.25 ± 12.86 PR status negative 9 104.11 ± 20.59 .032 – positive 27 142.21 ± 7.80 PR (%) <20 10 105.36 ± 20.15 .036 ≥20 26 142.21 ± 7.80 HER-2 Negative 31 128.45 ± 9.95 .403 – Positive 5 37-126.2 a Ki-67 (%) <20 19 131.69 ± 12.12 .951 ≥20 17 127.40 ± 12.42 Grade ≤2 19 143.17 ± 6.99 .405 – 3 15 126.39 ± 14.38 Invasion status No 15 130.48 ± 12.76 .878 – Yes 21 131.38 ± 11.64 Necrosis No 18 142.69 ± 7.44 .216 – Yes 16 99.01 ± 12.86 Immunohistochemical positive staining Synaptophysin 12 135.91 ± 11.47 .755 – Synaptophysin and chromogranin 22 135.93 ± 9.34 Loss of IHC BRCA immune expression No 11 11.1-73.2 a .126 – Yes 6 67.96 ± 13.55 Pathological type Luminal A 11 132.40 ± 16.10 .781 – Luminal B 23 128.51 ± 10.84 Stage ≤2 29 138.90 ± 7.77 .078 – 3 7 101.55 ± 25.09 Breast surgery BCS 13 18.6-148.8 a .037 – TM 23 109.68 ± 16.07 Tumor size (cm) <2 (1) 12 35.7-150.4 a .019 1-2:0.156 1-3: 0.016 2-3:0.096 ≥2 veya < 5 (2) 19 127.96 ± 13.48 ≥5 (3) 5 61.34 ± 15.43 TIL (%) ≤5 25 127.26 ± 10.38 .434 – >5 8 11.1-71.8 a Evaluated lymph nodes ≤3 16 132.94 ± 11.36 .497 – 3 > and ≤ 10 14 117.31 ± 19.49 10> 6 19.6-148.8 a Malignant lymph node state No 16 131.05 ± 12.50 .788 – Yes 17 123.64 ± 16.55 Number of malignant lymph node <4 26 136.93 ± 9.04 .058 – ≥4 7 57.24 ± 8.89 Neoadjuvant state No 27 123.78 ± 11.13 .221 – Yes 9 15-146.5 a Radiotherapy No 12 135.91 ± 11.47 .715 – Yes 24 127.86 ± 11.56 Adjuvant chemotherapy No 17 132.38 ± 11.80 .856 – Yes 19 127.41 ± 13.69 Open in a new tab Bold values indicate statistically significant differences. a Since all the cases were alive, survival time could not be calculated, min-max follow-up time (months) specified. Abbreviations: BRCA, breast cancer gene; ECOG PS, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Status; ER, estrogen receptor; HER-2, epidermal growth factor receptor 2; IHC, immunohistochemistry; Ki-67, proliferation index; PR, progesterone receptor; TIL, tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. A statistical difference was determined in DFS according to ECOG PS, stage, type of breast surgery, and number of malignant lymph nodes. Patients with an ECOG PS of ≤ 1 had longer DFS than those with an ECOG PS of 2 ( P = .002). Patients with fewer than 4 malignant lymph nodes had longer DFS than those with 4 or more ( P = <.001). Disease-free survival was longer in Stage 1 or 2 patients compared to Stage 3 patients ( P = .030). The DFS for patients who underwent mastectomy was 108.67 ± 16.51 months. There was no recurrence of patients who underwent BCS, and the follow-up period ranged from 18.6 to 148.8 months ( P = .032). No statistically significant differences were found in DFS in respect of other variables ( Table 3 ). Table 3. Comparison of DFS times. n Mean ± SD P -value Age <70 years 25 135.10 ± 9.18 .314 ≥70 years 11 118.67 ± 18.96 Menopausal status Premenopause 8 130.38 ± 17.04 .845 Postmenopause 28 131.19 ± 10.15 Comorbid disease No 11 134.47 ± 13.50 .819 Yes 25 128.54 ± 11.42 Number of comorbid disease ≤1 15 138.88 ± 9.52 .420 ≥2 21 122.74 ± 14.11 ECOG PS ≤1 32 139.45 ± 7.41 .002 2 4 41.06 ± 1.58 Tumor location Right 17 123.52 ± 13.65 .394 Left 19 108.52 ± 6.74 ER (%) <90 12 118.78 ± 15.66 .214 ≥ 90 24 138.98 ± 8.53 PR status negative 9 116.50 ± 16.62 .254 positive 27 136.01 ± 9.50 PR (%) <20 10 116.50 ± 19.62 .254 ≥20 26 136.01 ± 9.50 HER-2 Negative 31 127.34 ± 10.39 .371 Positive 5 37-126.2 a Ki-67 (%) <20 19 141.27 ± 8.73 .253 ≥20 17 117.64 ± 14.53 Grade ≤2 19 131.77 ± 12.35 .739 3 15 137.70 ± 10.53 İnvasion status No 15 126.56 ± 15.16 .841 Yes 21 131.81 ± 11.14 Necrosis No 18 139.93 ± 9.93 .132 Yes 16 97.76 ± 13.81 Pathological staining Synaptophysin 12 133.67 ± 13.40 .787 Synaptophysin and Chromogranin 22 128.61 ± 11.25 Loss of IHC BRCA immune expression No 11 72.83 ± 7.07 .296 Yes 6 67.10 ± 11.93 Pathological type Luminal a 11 132.15 ± 16.60 .802 Luminal b 23 125.88 ± 12.05 Stage ≤2 29 139.77 ± 7.19 .030 3 7 77.70 ± 28.11 Breast surgery BCS 13 18.6-148.8 a .032 TM 23 108.67 ± 16.51 Tumor size (cm) <2 (1) 12 139.52 ± 10.41 .387 ≥2 veya < 5 (2) 19 126.99 ± 13.87 ≥5 (3) 5 65.90 ± 17.67 TIL (%) ≤5 25 138.37 ± 8.03 .404 >5 8 46.70 ± 0.40 Number of lymph removed ≤3 16 141.73 ± 8.32 .523 3 > and ≤ 10 14 115.09 ± 19.40 10> 6 123.02 ± 23.05 Malignant lymph node state No 16 140.16 ± 9.76 .258 Yes 17 114.28 ± 16.76 Number of malignant lymph node <4 26 144.14 ± 6.07 <.001 ≥4 7 37.45 ± 6.39 Neoadjuvant state No 27 135.5 ± 8.82 .325 Yes 9 115.76 ± 20.80 Radiotherapy No 12 127.46 ± 18.28 .867 Yes 24 131.05 ± 10.24 Adjuvant chemotherapy No 17 141.97 ± 8.09 .191 Yes 19 116.97 ± 15.92 Open in a new tab Bold values indicate statistically significant differences. a Since all the cases were alive, survival time could not be calculated, min-max follow-up time (months) specified. Abbreviations: BCS, breast-conserving surgery; BRCA, breast cancer gene; ECOG PS, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Status; ER, estrogen receptor; HER-2, epidermal growth factor receptor 2; IHC, immunohistochemistry; Ki-67, proliferation index; PR, progesterone receptor; TIL, tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes; TM, total mastectomy. Twenty-four of 36 nonmetastatic BC patients received radiotherapy in the adjuvant period. There was no difference in DFS or OS among these patients ( P = 0.867 and 0.715, respectively) ( Tables 2 and 3 ). The patients were categorized into pathological groups based on synaptophysin or chromogranin staining. Both chromogranin and synaptophysin positivity were determined in 22 patients; 12 had only synaptophysin positivity, and 2 had only chromogranin positivity. A comparison was made between the chromogranin and synaptophysin-stained groups and the synaptophysin-only stained group. There was a statistically significant difference in PR value and ki-67 value based on pathological staining. The synaptophysin and chromogranin-stained group had a significantly higher PR value compared to the synaptophysin-stained group, but a lower ki-67 value. No statistically significant differences were found in the pathological staining according to the other variables ( Table 4 ). Pathological staining images are shown in Figure 3 . Table 4. Comparisons according to pathological staining. Synaptophysin Synaptophysin and chromogranin P -value n Median (Min-Max) n Median (Min-Max) Stage 12 1.5 (1-3) 22 1.5 (1-3) .901 TIL 11 5 (5-30) 21 5 (5-70) .815 ER 12 92.5 (0-100) 22 92.5 (0-100) .683 PR 12 35 (0-90) 22 87.5 (0-100) .031 Ki-67 12 27.5 (12-60) 22 13.5 (1-70) .023 Malignant lymph node 11 0 (0-8) 20 1 (0-8) .583 Tumor size 12 2.75 (0.7-9) 22 2.35 (0.6-13) .631 Number of lymph removed 12 3.5 (0-13) 22 5.5 (0-37) .345 Open in a new tab Bold values indicate statistically significant differences. Abbreviations: ER, estrogen receptor; Ki-67, proliferation index; PR, progesterone receptor; TIL, tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Pathological imagings of patients. (A) Diffuse, strong staining with synaptophysin in NET (×200). (B) Staining with chromogranin in NET (×200). (C) NET showed a growth pattern of solid islands (H&E ×100). (D) Absence of tubule formation, Nucleoli are not prominent in tumor cells, and fine chromatin is observed (H&E ×200). (E) Focal staining with chromogranin in NET (×200). (F) Diffuse, strong staining with synaptophysin in NET (×200). NACT was applied to 8 patients, and the treatment response rates of these patients were not related to the presence of necrosis ( P = .343). No statistically significant relationship was determined between the degree of NACT response and age at diagnosis, tumor size, ki-67, ER, PR, stage, and grade ( Table S2 ). Discussion The 5-year OS in the current study was 87.5%, and the 5-year DFS was 82.8%. In our study, there were 31 patients with known NET and 5 patients with NEC, and unlike previous studies, survival was better. In a population-based study using the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database, 239 NET, 122 NEC, and 491 908 IBC-NST patients were compared according to the 2019 WHO classification. The 5-year disease-specific survival rates were 63.39%, 46.00%, and 89.17%, respectively, and OS was 55.66%, 38.87%, and 83.17%, respectively. 8 In the study by Martinez et al. evaluating 1,389 neuroendocrine BC patients, the 5-year survival rates of these patients were 80%, 63%, and 45% for stages 1, 2, and 3, respectively. 9 In another study using the 2012 classification, NEC patients had shorter DFS than IBC-NST patients but similar OS. 10 Primary breast NETs are known to have high levels of ER and PR, HER-2 negativity, and low ki-67. 11 The current study population conformed to this generalization, with 5 patients having HER-2-positive disease, one having triple-negative immunohistochemical features, and only 2 patients having negative hormone receptors (%5.6). However, in a retrospective cohort analysis comparing 1389 breast NET patients with other BCs; NET patients were to be more likely hormone negative than the IBC-NST group (ER negativity: 32%-20%; PR negativity: 37%-29%). 9 In the current study, DFS for patients with stage 1 or 2 was longer than for patients with stage 3, but there was no impact on OS. Nottingham grade differences did not contribute to DFS or OS. The results of the current study showed that younger age (< 70 years), good ECOG PS, PR positivity, high PR, BCS, and small tumor diameter contributed to OS. In addition, having a good ECOG PS, being at the early stage, having BCS and having fewer than 4 malignant lymph nodes contributed to DFS. When comparing the pathological staining of the synaptophysin staining group and the group with both synaptophysin and chromogranin staining, it was observed that the group with both stainings had lower ki-67 values and higher PR rates. This suggests that the group with both stainings may have a better prognosis. Synaptophysin is a synaptic protein that plays a role in the endocytosis of synaptic vesicles. It has high sensitivity and specificity in neuroendocrine tumors. 12 Chromogranin a is a granin protein that has less sensitivity than synaptophysin in neuroendocrine tumors but has high specificity. 13 , 14 Chromogranin protein is known to be bactericidal and antifungal in inflammation, vasodilator in the cardiovascular system, regulator in calcium metabolism, and inducer of apoptosis. 15 In a study evaluating 1073 BC patients, synaptophysin positivity was found to be 4.9% and more advanced stage, higher grade, and less ER positive rate were associated with the disease. In the same cohort, chromogranin a positivity was associated only with loss of ER positivity. However, no difference in overall survival was found. 14 In a review published in 2021, it was stated that low synaptophysin and chromogranin levels may be associated with poor prognosis. 16 However, studies on this subject have yielded contradictory results, making a clear interpretation difficult. Although patients with both markers were positive in our study had a better prognosis, there is no clear information on the subject in the literature and no clear comment can be made on the explanatory mechanisms. When making decisions regarding adjuvant or neoadjuvant treatment in IBC-NST patients, factors such as the immunohistochemical structure of the tumor (ER, PR, HER-2, and Ki-67), tumor diameter, grade, axillary lymph node metastasis, and age should be evaluated. 16 Tumor diameter and nodal status are known to be determining factors for recurrence in breast NENs. 16 , 17 NETs do not respond well to chemotherapy, while NEC patients respond well. Some cases have utilized etoposide and platinum or anthracycline-containing chemotherapies in the neoadjuvant-adjuvant treatment of breast NENs. 16 , 18 In the current study, only one patient diagnosed with NEC received neoadjuvant etoposide and platinum therapy, but the response grade could not be evaluated. Of the 8 patients receiving other neoadjuvant therapies, only one patient had a pathological response of grade 4 according to the Miller-Payne grading system. The responses of the other patients were grade 3 and below. Interestingly, the patient with a grade 4 response was diagnosed with NET and received anthracycline-containing chemotherapy. The other patients diagnosed with NEC received platinum and etoposide treatments in the adjuvant period. The NACT response in this study was not correlated with age, tumor size, Ki-67, ER, PR, stage, or grade. There were 7 patients (23.3%) who had adjuvant cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor indication due to high risk and none of them received adjuvant CDK inhibitors. Although none of the patients received CDK inhibitors, the 5-year DFS in our cohort was 82.8%, which appears comparable to the MONARCH-E study population (83.6% with abemaciclib), though patient selection criteria and treatment background differ. 19 In early stage breast NEN, it is recommended to follow a treatment approach similar to IBC-NST, including surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, hormonal therapy, and anti-HER-2 therapy. 16 In the current study, the patients were in an early stage and were treated similarly to classical BC. Hormone receptor-positive patients received hormonal therapy, and HER-2-positive patients received anti-HER-2 therapy. As an exception, 4 patients diagnosed with NEC received etoposide and platinum. The strength of the current study lies in the fact that all patients since 2010 have been reevaluated and re-diagnosed with NET or NEC according to the latest WHO classification. Conclusion Primary NENs are extremely rare among both BCs and other NENs, and there are conflicting results regarding prognosis and treatment. In most of the other retrospective studies designed according to the new classification, patients were screened based on diagnosis codes. Our study can be considered as one of the rare studies in which preparations were reevaluated pathologically after the new classification. There is a need for further studies to be conducted on patients diagnosed according to the evolving classification. In our study, the prognosis of breast NEN patients was better than the literature data. Maybe the prognosis of patients evaluated as breast NEN according to the new classification is better than the older classification groups. Supplementary Material oyaf171_suppl_Supplementary_Tables_S1-S3 oyaf171_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1-s3.docx (21.3KB, docx) Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank Dr. İlker Ercan (Department of Biostatistics, Uludag University, Bursa, Turkey) for the precious help with sample size calculation. Contributor Information Seda Sali, Department of Medical Oncology, Bursa City Hospital, 16110 Nilufer, Turkey. Mursel Sali, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Burcu Caner, Department of Medical Oncology, Aydin Ataturk State Hospital, 09100 Aydin, Turkey. Birol Ocak, Department of Medical Oncology, Bursa Yuksek Ihtisas Training and Research Hospital, 16310 Yildirim, Turkey. Eyup Coban, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Alper Coskun, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Mine Ozsen, Department of Pathology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Sibel Oyucu Orhan, Department of Medical Oncology, Bursa City Hospital, 16110 Nilufer, Turkey. Ahmet Bilgehan Sahin, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Adem Deligonul, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Erdem Cubukcu, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Sahsine Tolunay, Department of Pathology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Turkkan Evrensel, Department of Medical Oncology, School of Medicine, Bursa Uludag University, 16059 Gorukle, Turkey. Author contributions Dr. S.S., B.O., and A.B.S. contributed to the conception of study and S.S., A.D., E.C., and T.E. to design of the work. S.S., M.S., E.Co., A.C., and S.O.O. contributed to acquisition and analysis of study data and provided technical material support. S.S., B.C., M.O., and S.T. conducted the literature review and contributed to interpretation of data. S.S., A.D., E.C., and T.E. have drafted the work and substantively revised it. S.S. wrote the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding None declared. Conflicts of interest None declared. Data availability The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the contributing author on reasonable request. [Seda Sali/seda_ist1987@hotmail.com/sedasali@uludag.edu.tr]. References 1. Feyrter F, Hartmann G.. On the carcinoid growth form of the carcinoma mammae, especially the carcinoma solidum (gelatinosum) mammae. Frankf Z Pathol. 1963;73:24-39. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Rakha EA, Reis-Filho JS, Sasano H, Wu Y.. Neuroendocrine neoplasms: introduction. WHO Classification Tumours Breast Tumours. 2019;2:155. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Hörsch D, Schmid KW, Anlauf M, et al. Neuroendocrine tumors of the bronchopulmonary system (typical and atypical carcinoid tumors): current strategies in diagnosis and treatment. Conclusions of an expert meeting February 2011 in Weimar, Germany. Oncol Res Treat. 2014;37:266-276. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Tsang JY, Gary MT.. Breast cancer with neuroendocrine differentiation: an update based on the latest WHO classification. Mod Pathol. 2021;34:1062-1073. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tan PH, Ellis I, Allison K, et al. The 2019 WHO classification of tumours of the breast. Histopathology. 2020;77:181. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Dasari A, Shen C, Halperin D, et al. Trends in the incidence, prevalence, and survival outcomes in patients with neuroendocrine tumors in the United States. JAMA Oncol. 2017;3:1335-1342. https://doi.org/ 10.1001/jamaoncol.2017.0589 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Lai BS, Tsang JY, Poon IK, et al. The clinical significance of neuroendocrine features in invasive breast carcinomas. Oncologist. 2020;25:e1318-e1329. https://doi.org/ 10.1634/theoncologist.2020-0081 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ya